WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.880
- Welcome, everyone

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.330
to the first of our
two-part webinar series

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:15.120
on the 2022 midterm elections

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.130
sponsored by Yale's Institution

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.980
for Social and Policy Studies,

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.363
which is known as ISPS.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.940
My name is Alan Gerber.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.860
I'm a professor at Yale

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.283
in the Department of Political
Science and Director of ISPS.

00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:32.340
And today I'm very
excited to share with you

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.222
the insights of our expert panel,

00:00:34.222 --> 00:00:36.543
and I'll be introducing them shortly.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.210
So first, I just wanna encourage you,

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.470
there's a good amount
of time in the webinar

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.210
for question and answer.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.818
And so please send questions for the panel

00:00:47.818 --> 00:00:52.080
at any time through the
Q&amp;A button at the bottom

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.499
of your Zoom screen.

00:00:53.499 --> 00:00:55.507
And please try,

00:00:55.507 --> 00:00:58.830
we're gonna try and read
the questions that you ask

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.480
to capture your own voice.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:01.857
And so we'll be able to do that

00:01:01.857 --> 00:01:04.870
if you keep the questions brief

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:09.630
and in a form that's easy for us to field

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.900
to our colleagues on the panel.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.390
And I just wanna also
say that today's event

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.306
is being recorded and
we'll be posting the link

00:01:18.306 --> 00:01:21.180
of the video as soon as possible.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.110
And then also for those
of you who are on Twitter,

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.757
our handle is @ispsyale.

00:01:28.483 --> 00:01:33.483
And you can also use
the #ispsmidterms2022.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.357
I wanna begin by thanking the staff,

00:01:38.357 --> 00:01:40.530
the people in the Yale community

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.165
who really make all the events

00:01:43.165 --> 00:01:46.080
that we do here at ISPS possible.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.480
I wanna give special thanks to Pam Greene,

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.919
Rick Harrison, Limor Peer,

00:01:50.919 --> 00:01:54.150
and Kira Wishart for all they do

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:58.966
to help ISPS fulfill its
research and education mission.

00:01:58.966 --> 00:02:02.310
And then the rhythm of the
event tonight will be this.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:06.303
The panelists will speak
for five to seven minutes.

00:02:06.303 --> 00:02:08.130
When they're finished,

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:11.310
we're gonna have a discussion
among the panelists

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.740
for maybe 20 minutes or so,

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.000
and then that'll be followed

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.200
by a question and answer period

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.474
where the questions that
you've been sending us

00:02:23.474 --> 00:02:26.760
during the panel phase

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:31.350
will be fielded to the panelists.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.480
And with that, I just wanna
introduce today's panel

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.340
who will be presenting
in alphabetical order.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.890
So first, Mo Fiorina.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.200
Oh, these are gonna be
brief introductions,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.320
because I just wanna leave
as much time as possible

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.700
for the presentations, discussion,
and question and answer.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.820
So, and these are very
distinguished people,

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.050
so it could go on for a while

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:57.690
if I went through the whole thing.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.440
Okay, so Mo Fiorina is
a professor at Stanford.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.110
He's the Wendt Family
professor of political science,

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:06.180
author of many books,

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:10.710
and the member of many
distinguished academic societies,

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.766
including the American
Academy of Arts and Sciences

00:03:13.766 --> 00:03:16.980
and the National Academy of Sciences.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.942
Josh Kalla is our second presenter.

00:03:19.942 --> 00:03:22.392
Josh is an assistant professor

00:03:22.392 --> 00:03:25.170
of political science at Yale

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.510
and is also in the
Department of Statistics

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:28.778
and Data Science,

00:03:28.778 --> 00:03:32.580
and a resident fellow of ISPS.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.800
He works on political persuasion,

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.920
prejudice reduction,
decision making among voters

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.301
and political elites,

00:03:39.301 --> 00:03:41.718
and he's won a number of awards

00:03:41.718 --> 00:03:43.710
to recognize the scholarship,

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:48.710
including the Leamer-Rosenthal
for Open Social Science.

00:03:49.648 --> 00:03:53.572
Mike Podhorzer is the
former political director

00:03:53.572 --> 00:03:55.492
of the AFL-CIO.

00:03:55.492 --> 00:03:59.417
He's also the founding chair
of the Analyst Institute

00:03:59.417 --> 00:04:04.417
and one of the most important
institutional actors

00:04:05.151 --> 00:04:09.390
in progressive politics
over the past few decades.

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:13.350
We're extremely privileged
to have him joining us today.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.273
And then finally, Lynn Vavreck.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.463
Lynn is the Marvin Hoffenberg Professor

00:04:18.463 --> 00:04:22.486
of American Politics and
Public Policy at UCLA.

00:04:22.486 --> 00:04:26.130
Lynn has been a pioneer in many areas

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:28.530
in American political behavior.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:31.077
Recently in 2020,

00:04:31.077 --> 00:04:34.874
she led the Nationscape Election Project,

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:37.655
the largest study of
presidential elections

00:04:37.655 --> 00:04:40.680
ever fielded in the United States.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.510
She's also the recipient
of numerous awards

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:47.010
and the author of numerous books.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.410
Her awards include the
Andrew Carnegie Prize

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.170
in the Humanities and Social Sciences.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:55.170
And with that, let me turn
it over to our panelists

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:57.483
who will speak in alphabetic order.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.330
- Okay, well, our instructions,

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:03.120
Professor Gerber asked us not to make more

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.515
than two or three arguments.

00:05:04.515 --> 00:05:05.820
I'll do better than that.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.468
I'm not gonna make any arguments.

00:05:07.468 --> 00:05:10.631
I'll give you one not so courageous guess.

00:05:10.631 --> 00:05:13.800
And the reason for that is,
normally after every election,

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.212
there is a narrative that
takes hold about what happened.

00:05:16.212 --> 00:05:18.510
And often the narrative is wrong

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:20.385
when we actually do our analyses.

00:05:20.385 --> 00:05:22.543
But at least you have
something to argue against.

00:05:22.543 --> 00:05:25.080
In this election, there
are just any number

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:26.787
of competing narratives out there.

00:05:26.787 --> 00:05:28.776
The election, depending
on who you talk to,

00:05:28.776 --> 00:05:31.756
was about abortion, or was
about election denying,

00:05:31.756 --> 00:05:34.870
or was about January 6th
and the threat to democracy,

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:37.317
and the critical groups or independents

00:05:37.317 --> 00:05:40.068
or young people or suburban residents

00:05:40.068 --> 00:05:44.234
or women or young women or
young college educated women.

00:05:44.234 --> 00:05:47.040
And I'm not gonna go
to any of these places.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.590
I think we have some real data
gurus on the program here,

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.956
and I'll let them

00:05:51.956 --> 00:05:54.810
have all those kinds of questions.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:57.000
After the 2020 election,

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.937
which was disappointing for
the Democrats in the House,

00:05:59.937 --> 00:06:01.335
not the presidency obviously,

00:06:01.335 --> 00:06:04.280
but in the House they lost
13 seats, surprisingly.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.530
And their margin was only, I believe nine.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:09.248
And virtually every political
scientist in the country

00:06:09.248 --> 00:06:11.347
said after the election, the next day,

00:06:11.347 --> 00:06:14.940
"They're gonna lose the House
in the next election, 2022."

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:16.983
And let me start sharing my screen here.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:20.940
You've probably all heard
this generalization,

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:23.190
but if you've never
actually seen the numbers,

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:24.949
this is a chart of midterm losses

00:06:24.949 --> 00:06:27.570
from the Civil War on.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:30.690
1900, 1902 doesn't really count there.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.030
The size of the House was increased

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:33.990
and everybody gained seats,

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:35.430
although the incumbent party

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:37.200
didn't get as many as the others.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:38.896
But basically won the
election, the New Deal.

00:06:38.896 --> 00:06:41.700
And then not until Newt
Gingrich doubled down

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:44.187
on the Monica Lewinsky scandal in 1998,

00:06:44.187 --> 00:06:48.827
in the aftermath of
2001 terrorist attacks,

00:06:48.827 --> 00:06:52.200
did any incumbent party gain seats.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:54.690
You can see the gain is
played below the line there.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:57.540
The only question is
how many seats you lose?

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.270
Now why is it always below the line?

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:02.220
Well, there are a number
of theories out there,

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.530
but I basically think it's the idea

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:08.411
of a thermostatic electorate
is what explains it.

00:07:08.411 --> 00:07:11.100
That neither party has a
majority of the country,

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:12.090
and to win in the election,

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:14.040
you basically have to appeal

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:15.780
to middle of the road-ers

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:17.591
unaffiliated, moderates, et cetera.

00:07:17.591 --> 00:07:20.010
But then you govern basically according

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:21.690
to the dictates of your base.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:23.790
And a certain number of
people who voted for you

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:26.320
in the previous election decide
that's not what they wanted.

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:27.610
And here's an example.

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:31.077
This is Barack Obama in November, 2008.

00:07:31.077 --> 00:07:32.820
Now the country was about evenly split

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:36.600
on whether it'll elect
a moderate or liberal,

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.882
by having watched cap and trade,

00:07:38.882 --> 00:07:42.060
medical care, Obamacare, et cetera.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:43.890
About a quarter of the people
who thought he was a moderate

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:45.970
decided a year later, no,
they elected a liberal.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:48.887
Hence, the schlacking
that occurred after that.

00:07:48.887 --> 00:07:51.261
Political scientists have
developed forecasting models

00:07:51.261 --> 00:07:53.340
to predict the seat losses.

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:56.103
They're basically models that
predict statistical models

00:07:56.103 --> 00:07:57.941
based on economic conditions.

00:07:57.941 --> 00:08:00.300
Presidential approval sucks up everything

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:01.301
other than the economy.

00:08:01.301 --> 00:08:03.450
And there are variants
like, are we at war?

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:04.764
Are there war casualties?

00:08:04.764 --> 00:08:06.262
How many seats do you have exposed

00:08:06.262 --> 00:08:09.022
beyond your long-term average?

00:08:09.022 --> 00:08:10.590
How long have you been in office?

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:11.670
And so forth.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:13.920
And those models
sometimes work quite well.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:15.210
And 2020, for example,

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.700
the average of the models was exactly on.

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.440
Now in 2022, those kind of models

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:21.838
were predicting unbelievable figures.

00:08:21.838 --> 00:08:25.290
Seth Masket would
continually update these,

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:27.660
and he was always getting
figures like 60 seats.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.919
And he, of course, didn't
believe that. No one else did.

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:31.706
Jim Campbell was one
of the few forecasters

00:08:31.706 --> 00:08:34.753
who was willing to go public
and he said about 40 seats.

00:08:34.753 --> 00:08:37.800
I checked with some of the
other forecasters informally,

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:40.560
and basically they were
reluctant to go public

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:43.680
because the models simply
seem to be making predictions

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:46.063
outside what was expected.

00:08:46.063 --> 00:08:47.923
I think like many of the other panelists,

00:08:47.923 --> 00:08:50.070
we had to give various talks.

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:51.450
And my pre-election prediction

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:54.397
to the Stanford newspaper
and other venues was,

00:08:54.397 --> 00:08:57.976
I'd be very shocked if
anything other than something

00:08:57.976 --> 00:09:00.437
between 49 to 51 seats for either party

00:09:00.437 --> 00:09:02.400
occurred in the Senate,

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:04.510
and certainly no more than
30 seats in the House,

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:06.324
20 to 30 seats.

00:09:06.324 --> 00:09:08.360
And the reason for the caution,

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:11.130
obviously, one, were fewer marginal seats,

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:14.302
fewer seats in the danger zone
after the last redistricting.

00:09:14.302 --> 00:09:17.070
Second, Republicans already
hold a lot of seats.

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:18.320
I didn't see a lot of discussion of this

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:19.756
in the run up to the election.

00:09:19.756 --> 00:09:23.925
In 1994, the Republican
wave of 54 seats in 2010,

00:09:23.925 --> 00:09:26.883
the Democrats were lacking 63 seats.

00:09:26.883 --> 00:09:29.837
In 2022, they had three
dozen more seats going in.

00:09:29.837 --> 00:09:32.429
So it was really unlikely that
they were gonna have anything

00:09:32.429 --> 00:09:35.040
like a 40 to 50 seat gain,

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.218
which is what Kevin McCarthy said.

00:09:37.218 --> 00:09:39.023
The third thing was, of course, Dobbs,

00:09:39.023 --> 00:09:42.000
and several of the forecasters
I talked to said simply

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.710
they just simply didn't know how

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:46.360
to take account of that in their models.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:48.712
And frankly, you always have to count

00:09:48.712 --> 00:09:50.940
Republican primary voters.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:52.770
Just to go back on some
of the golden oldies

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.549
from the past, in 2010,

00:09:54.549 --> 00:09:56.792
Christine O'Donnell had to run commercials

00:09:56.792 --> 00:09:58.957
denying that she was a witch.

00:09:58.957 --> 00:10:02.580
In 2012, we had a number
of comments about rape

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:06.303
that struck people and
was beyond the mainstream.

00:10:10.032 --> 00:10:13.440
The Republican candidate
who ran against Bamberger

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:17.310
in Virginia actually got caught
in this argument this year.

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:19.140
She sort of sensed the danger,

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:22.080
but she basically

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.270
in one campaign appearance,

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:26.880
she actually agreed with this,

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.790
and it haunted her for
the rest of the campaign.

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:32.040
The all-time favorite,
I think, was this one.

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:35.910
These are, of course, Senate elections,

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:38.749
and we had a few candidates who were,

00:10:38.749 --> 00:10:41.580
as Mitch McConnell said, not high quality.

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:44.610
This time I wasn't sure that'd
actually go to the House,

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:45.507
but we did have some

00:10:45.507 --> 00:10:47.880
real sort of surprising candidates

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:49.560
in the House races as well.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:51.450
So in summary, the
Republicans were likely to win

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:54.240
the House majority, not as
big as conditions suggest.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:55.969
Senate majority was uncertain.

00:10:55.969 --> 00:10:59.040
In the end, they gained only
nine seats in the House,

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:00.450
they gained only two governorships

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:02.400
and they lost a Senate seat.

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:05.095
And the story basically is the
independents didn't deliver

00:11:05.095 --> 00:11:06.300
for the Republicans.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:07.716
When you look back over time,

00:11:07.716 --> 00:11:09.584
independents sort of determined the size

00:11:09.584 --> 00:11:11.191
of these midterm losses.

00:11:11.191 --> 00:11:15.540
Here's the Reagan era here.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:17.700
Here's the Republican wave in '94.

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:19.413
Here's the Democrats slumping,

00:11:20.731 --> 00:11:22.650
and they're really slumping in 2006,

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:26.450
and the Republicans
slacking in 2014, 2018.

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:29.220
The independents actually voted 2 or 3%,

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:30.822
probably before, according to some polls

00:11:30.822 --> 00:11:32.514
for the Democrats this time.

00:11:32.514 --> 00:11:35.580
So it was the independents
didn't have a very close margin.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:37.430
They actually went for the Democrats.

00:11:38.282 --> 00:11:41.609
And the last few elections
have not been kind

00:11:41.609 --> 00:11:43.670
to the idea of a new Democratic majority

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:45.755
or the emerging Democratic majority.

00:11:45.755 --> 00:11:48.513
People have put together
these from the Exit Polls

00:11:48.513 --> 00:11:50.389
and the AP Poll.

00:11:50.389 --> 00:11:53.664
African-Americans continue
to defect, to some extent,

00:11:53.664 --> 00:11:58.029
Hispanics defect, working
class, suburban, old people.

00:11:58.029 --> 00:12:01.071
Young people on the other
hand, more Democratic.

00:12:01.071 --> 00:12:05.154
I really like Ruy Teixeira,
his blog, the Liberal Patriot.

00:12:05.154 --> 00:12:08.010
He's a bit pessimistic on these things.

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:10.070
He likes to try to find the dark lining

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:11.940
in every silver cloud.

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:14.067
But his last blog the other day,

00:12:14.067 --> 00:12:16.753
he's pointing out all the disadvantages

00:12:16.753 --> 00:12:18.329
that the Democrats face,

00:12:18.329 --> 00:12:21.240
even though they did find in the election,

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:22.551
like on immigration, the border,

00:12:22.551 --> 00:12:24.930
they're 24 points down Republicans.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:27.120
Crime, 20, economy and abortion,

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:29.820
20, or excuse me, economy, 20,

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:31.230
that should be inflation.

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:33.480
Abortion rights, they're
up a little of course,

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:35.280
and climate change, which
is not an important issue

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:36.390
to most people.

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:38.280
And of course on
traditional values, again,

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:41.460
the Democrats are the
disadvantage on these things.

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:42.810
So he sees dangers ahead

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:46.758
no matter what, how they managed to escape

00:12:46.758 --> 00:12:49.170
oblivion in the last election.

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:52.050
And my guess is based on all this,

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:55.950
is that in general everything mattered.

00:12:55.950 --> 00:12:58.525
Everything sort of added
up to the perception

00:12:58.525 --> 00:13:00.450
that the Republicans were too extreme

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:01.770
and beyond the mainstream.

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:03.774
It was a little bit of all the things.

00:13:03.774 --> 00:13:07.275
There was a nice third
way pre-election survey

00:13:07.275 --> 00:13:09.960
in which people were
asked to place the parties

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.550
on the ideological spectrum.

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:12.931
And what was interesting is they actually

00:13:12.931 --> 00:13:16.950
judged the parties to be equal,

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:19.020
not actually, judged the Democratic party

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:20.520
to be more extreme ideologically

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:21.870
than the Republican party.

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:24.300
But when asked about the
current candidates this year,

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:26.100
no, they felt the
Republicans had nominated

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:27.795
more extreme candidates this year.

00:13:27.795 --> 00:13:29.160
So I'll end with that

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:31.460
and allow my fellow
panelists to flesh it out.

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:35.880
- Okay, so I think Mo and I,

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:37.470
as I was saying, we did not coordinate

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:38.760
in what we were talking about,

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:40.680
but really similar questions

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:42.914
and also similar answers.

00:13:42.914 --> 00:13:45.570
But I think one question I have
coming out of this election

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:49.113
is did the Republicans
underperform in 2022?

00:13:49.113 --> 00:13:51.330
And in order to answer that question,

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:54.360
we need to have some model as to how well

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:57.441
the Republicans should
have performed in 2022.

00:13:57.441 --> 00:13:59.940
And one nice model out there,

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:01.770
there are many of these predictive models,

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:04.050
but one nice one is
from our Yale colleague

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:05.593
Ray Fair in the economics department,

00:14:05.593 --> 00:14:09.160
where he talks about these different

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:12.960
pushes and pulls for or
against the incumbent party.

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.060
So based on the fact

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:17.010
that there's a democratic incumbent

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:19.980
and there's some both bias
against the White House,

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:22.590
that thermostatic public opinion
that Moe was talking about

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:25.260
canceled out by some incumbency effect,

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:27.293
but also the economy was both bad

00:14:27.293 --> 00:14:30.428
with negative, very high inflation,

00:14:30.428 --> 00:14:33.003
but also was good with high GDP growth.

00:14:33.003 --> 00:14:34.980
All of this, when you
put this all together

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:36.258
in Ray Fair's model,

00:14:36.258 --> 00:14:39.007
means that based on past election results,

00:14:39.007 --> 00:14:41.520
we should have expected
the Republican vote share

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:44.584
in this election to be about 53.4%.

00:14:44.584 --> 00:14:48.090
And what we ended up seeing
at least as of November 23rd,

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:50.160
is that the actual Republican vote share

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.890
in this election was about 51.6%.

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:54.630
So this means that
Republicans underperformed

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:56.631
relative to these historic models

00:14:56.631 --> 00:15:00.960
based on the economy, based on incumbency,

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:03.000
based on how many seats they
were holding in the past.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:04.680
Republicans seem to underperform

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:06.610
by about two percentage points.

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:09.330
So it does seem like Republicans,

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:11.040
based on what we have known in the past,

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:12.720
based on what we have
seen from past elections,

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:14.426
should have done better
than they ended up doing

00:15:14.426 --> 00:15:15.988
this past November.

00:15:15.988 --> 00:15:19.322
So then the obvious question
to ask next is, why?

00:15:19.322 --> 00:15:22.709
And here I don't think we
have very solid answers.

00:15:22.709 --> 00:15:25.441
But there's some
political science evidence

00:15:25.441 --> 00:15:28.800
that we can point to suggest why it is

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:30.720
that Republicans might
have under-performed,

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:32.940
that Democrats might have over-performed.

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:34.740
And one piece of evidence we see

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:37.465
is that Democrats raised
and spent more money

00:15:37.465 --> 00:15:39.810
than Republicans this election.

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:42.380
So in US House races,

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:46.770
Democrats spent about
$408 million on TV ads,

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:49.410
while Republican candidates
and affiliated groups

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:52.856
spent about $380 million on their TV ads.

00:15:52.856 --> 00:15:56.532
And Lynn Vavreck, who's speaking later,

00:15:56.532 --> 00:15:59.393
along with a few colleagues,
John Sides and Chris Warshaw,

00:15:59.393 --> 00:16:00.907
have a really nice recent paper

00:16:00.907 --> 00:16:02.783
in the "American
Political Science Review,"

00:16:02.783 --> 00:16:06.000
showing that when one party

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:09.188
has an advertising advantage
over another party,

00:16:09.188 --> 00:16:12.240
this leads to an electoral impact.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:16.878
There is a benefit to spending
more ads than the other side.

00:16:16.878 --> 00:16:18.600
And it's a pretty significant effect,

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:21.210
especially in down ballot races

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:22.530
like the House of Representatives

00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:24.176
where Democrats out-performed

00:16:24.176 --> 00:16:25.650
what these models would predict.

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:28.950
So I think one explanation
as to why Republicans

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:31.500
under-performed is they
might've just spent less money

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:32.800
than the Democratic party.

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:36.390
Another explanation, which
Mo talked a little bit about,

00:16:36.390 --> 00:16:38.160
is about extremism,

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:40.480
about how Republican primaries

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:46.320
voted for candidates who
were just more extreme,

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:50.220
less high quality than who
the Democrats went for.

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:51.970
And again, there's really nice work

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:54.876
in political science by Andy Hall

00:16:54.876 --> 00:16:56.820
now at Stanford, for example,

00:16:56.820 --> 00:16:58.800
who looks at what happens when

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:01.470
more extreme candidates
win primary elections.

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:03.540
Is there an electoral penalty?

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:05.580
And one model of the win might say no,

00:17:05.580 --> 00:17:07.410
there is no electoral penalty.

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:09.300
All that matters in
American politics today

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:12.450
is whether you have a D
or an R next to your name.

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:13.620
Democrats vote for Democrats,

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:15.450
Republicans vote for Republicans.

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:17.100
There are very, very few independents,

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:20.593
and all that matters is the
partisanship of who's running.

00:17:20.593 --> 00:17:24.930
And that the extremism of
the individual candidates

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:25.920
does not matter.

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:29.280
And what Andy finds in this
paper is that view is wrong,

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:31.253
that individual candidates
do in fact matter.

00:17:31.253 --> 00:17:33.077
More extreme candidates are punished

00:17:33.077 --> 00:17:35.483
when it comes to the general election.

00:17:35.483 --> 00:17:38.068
And there does seem to be
some evidence from this

00:17:38.068 --> 00:17:39.894
in the most recent election.

00:17:39.894 --> 00:17:41.761
The "New York Times" did
a really nice analysis

00:17:41.761 --> 00:17:44.995
where they looked at US House races

00:17:44.995 --> 00:17:47.760
where there was a " MAGA Republican"

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:49.591
who won the primary election
and ran in the general,

00:17:49.591 --> 00:17:53.340
compared to all other Republican races.

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:55.650
And what you see when you just look at how

00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:59.469
these MAGA versus non MAGA
Republicans did in 2022

00:17:59.469 --> 00:18:01.300
relative to 2020,

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:04.680
is that the typical Republican,

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:06.120
the Chamber of Commerce Republican,

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:08.823
they actually did much better in 2022

00:18:08.823 --> 00:18:12.570
than they did in 2020,
by about six points.

00:18:12.570 --> 00:18:14.340
The issue for the Republican party

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:17.919
is that those MAGA Republicans
did much worse in 2022

00:18:17.919 --> 00:18:19.711
relative to the 2020 benchmark.

00:18:19.711 --> 00:18:22.650
They actually performed
about half a point worse.

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:25.550
So this really seems to suggest
that who the Republicans

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:28.920
ran in these elections might
have been a contributing factor

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:31.553
for the under-performance
of the Republican party.

00:18:31.553 --> 00:18:35.490
And then the third, I think obvious point,

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:37.800
but I think a really open
point, is this backlash

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:39.805
to overturning Roe v.
Wade, the Dobbs decision.

00:18:39.805 --> 00:18:42.204
And here I actually haven't seen any,

00:18:42.204 --> 00:18:43.890
I think, convincing evidence

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:46.380
showing how or why to what extent

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:47.400
that Dobbs really mattered.

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:50.070
I think it very likely did.

00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:51.630
But I think in just an open question,

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:53.010
I would love if the other panelists

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:53.843
have any thoughts on this,

00:18:53.843 --> 00:18:56.400
is what data would actually
test whether or not,

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:59.040
or how would we know whether
or not Dobbs mattered?

00:18:59.040 --> 00:18:59.873
'Cause likely it did.

00:18:59.873 --> 00:19:01.890
But it would be nice to
be able to triangulate

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:03.884
with some data around that.

00:19:03.884 --> 00:19:05.130
But that's it for me,

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:07.530
and I'll turn it over to Mike Podhorzer.

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:08.780
- [Michael] Thanks, Josh.

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:11.760
Actually gonna start in a
different place than I thought

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:16.030
because of how the
previous panelists started

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:18.720
before getting to my presentation.

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:20.771
And there's a couple, I know
Alan knows and Josh knows

00:19:20.771 --> 00:19:24.570
(indistinct) who have, like,
been getting some of my stuff

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:26.280
for the last couple of years.

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:29.580
I thought the Democrats actually could

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:31.320
hold the House this year,

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:35.040
and that I went through
sort of two long years

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:37.560
of eye-rolling at me.

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:40.039
And that's because I think
what ended up happening

00:19:40.039 --> 00:19:43.122
was that Republicans over-performed

00:19:43.122 --> 00:19:45.210
from a very different perspective

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:47.610
than the modeling we just heard, right?

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:50.902
If you, in November, 2008,

00:19:50.902 --> 00:19:53.760
right after Barack Obama won the election,

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:58.152
had been put in a medically induced coma

00:19:58.152 --> 00:20:00.840
and then revived now,

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:05.070
and were told that in 2020
Donald Trump had been president,

00:20:05.070 --> 00:20:06.257
lost the election,

00:20:06.257 --> 00:20:09.476
staged a violent coup attempt,

00:20:09.476 --> 00:20:11.400
and then the Republican party,

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:13.920
instead of distancing itself from him,

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:16.620
actually tripled down on him,

00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:18.552
that the result was that they actually

00:20:18.552 --> 00:20:21.643
took the House of Representatives, right?

00:20:21.643 --> 00:20:24.210
Your head would explode.

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:26.160
And I think sometimes we pay

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:27.660
sort of too much attention

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:31.470
to the sort of...

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:35.040
The data in a very sort of myopic way

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:38.070
and not understand that
we're in a different

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:41.463
political time since 2016.

00:20:41.463 --> 00:20:44.313
And that's what I'm gonna talk about.

00:20:45.591 --> 00:20:49.980
So as folks were saying,

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:53.160
the result was not what
the model suggested

00:20:53.160 --> 00:20:53.993
was gonna happen.

00:20:53.993 --> 00:20:56.122
It's not what people who were following it

00:20:56.122 --> 00:20:57.872
said were gonna happen.

00:20:57.872 --> 00:21:00.360
And it isn't just that it didn't happen.

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:04.027
It happened, it didn't
happen in a spectacular way.

00:21:04.027 --> 00:21:05.850
Since the Voting Rights Act,

00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:09.265
these were the seven midterms
in which the president

00:21:09.265 --> 00:21:11.204
was underwater and all the other things

00:21:11.204 --> 00:21:12.833
are sort of going wrong.

00:21:12.833 --> 00:21:16.380
There's only one where the
party picks up governor,

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:19.170
state legislative chambers,
has such a small loss.

00:21:19.170 --> 00:21:21.543
It's completely ahistorical.

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:26.550
And if you even drill down
a little bit more, right?

00:21:26.550 --> 00:21:29.400
It's as you just heard, nine seats lost

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:32.559
despite all those fundamentals
plus rising prices,

00:21:32.559 --> 00:21:36.250
crime, all of that, double digit losses.

00:21:36.250 --> 00:21:38.970
But if you go even
further, think about this.

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:41.748
In particular, Gretchen
Whitmer and Tony Evers,

00:21:41.748 --> 00:21:46.748
won reelection by more
in the red election,

00:21:47.961 --> 00:21:51.360
than they won in the blue wave, right?

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:55.464
And going back again to post-DRA,

00:21:55.464 --> 00:21:58.317
like, that's not ever happened.

00:21:58.317 --> 00:22:00.849
Democrats netting state
legislative chambers,

00:22:00.849 --> 00:22:03.045
that's never happened.

00:22:03.045 --> 00:22:06.390
Four democratic senators
were elected or reelected

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:09.120
from swing states.

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:13.290
This cycle, only three total

00:22:13.290 --> 00:22:16.188
in any midterm since 1994

00:22:16.188 --> 00:22:18.480
have been able to do that

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:20.771
against their president's party.

00:22:20.771 --> 00:22:25.771
It's an astonishingly different
political environment.

00:22:26.353 --> 00:22:31.080
And I think part of the
reason that people are,

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:32.390
we don't have a handle on it

00:22:32.390 --> 00:22:35.790
is because of the explanations we get

00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:39.930
for how this happened,
really don't match the data

00:22:39.930 --> 00:22:44.310
or the moment that they're kind
of procrustean explanations,

00:22:44.310 --> 00:22:47.460
swing voters, independents, all of that.

00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:51.330
And I don't have a, obviously,
a conclusive answer,

00:22:51.330 --> 00:22:54.297
but wanna offer a few observations

00:22:54.297 --> 00:22:57.200
about a different way to think about it.

00:22:57.200 --> 00:23:00.812
Right, so if procrustean,
right, is the idea is that

00:23:00.812 --> 00:23:05.560
you, to make sure that your guest

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:07.200
gets the bed comfortably,

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:09.990
just cut off what doesn't fit, right?

00:23:09.990 --> 00:23:12.720
And what our analysis of elections

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:14.820
since Trump had been doing is really

00:23:14.820 --> 00:23:18.391
cutting off the effect of
differential partisan turnout,

00:23:18.391 --> 00:23:20.550
the local political context

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:23.044
as opposed to race by race analysis.

00:23:23.044 --> 00:23:26.162
And what the media's saying
in each of these markets

00:23:26.162 --> 00:23:28.260
is really, it's only the media that gets

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:33.060
to really conclusively say
what's true and what's not.

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:36.750
And after 2016, right,

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:40.440
the turnout just became
something it never was, right?

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:43.313
Turnout went up by
almost 14 points in 2018,

00:23:43.313 --> 00:23:46.620
twice as much as it ever
had since reconstruction,

00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:50.160
which was twice as much as
it had ever gone before that.

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:52.067
We're just in a different turnout era.

00:23:52.067 --> 00:23:54.635
And so almost all the analysis we've read

00:23:54.635 --> 00:23:58.740
or heard about is about the
roughly 11 million people

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:01.830
who might be called swing
voters or independents

00:24:01.830 --> 00:24:05.312
and the electorate, and nothing
about the 45 million people

00:24:05.312 --> 00:24:10.312
who voted in 2020, but
stayed home this time, right?

00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:12.480
Which is a very different situation

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:14.520
from historic midterms.

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:19.520
In historic midterms, about
3/4 of the electorate or more

00:24:19.526 --> 00:24:21.690
were 4/4 voters.

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:25.590
And now that's down to
just under 50%, right?

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:27.297
There's just a big new pool of voters,

00:24:27.297 --> 00:24:29.158
and no one's paying attention to 'em.

00:24:29.158 --> 00:24:32.231
And that's where

00:24:32.231 --> 00:24:35.577
these shifts are happening, right?

00:24:35.577 --> 00:24:38.890
So the thing I want to put
out is that I don't think

00:24:38.890 --> 00:24:41.640
that this was a red mist.

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:44.490
I think we actually had two
midterms in the country.

00:24:44.490 --> 00:24:48.000
We had one midterm that was the red wave

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:49.931
that all those models were predicting,

00:24:49.931 --> 00:24:52.653
and I shouldn't say
wave, but blue undertow.

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:55.865
And so the metric that I'm
gonna use in these couple slides

00:24:55.865 --> 00:24:58.380
is presidential swing.

00:24:58.380 --> 00:24:59.790
In each of the districts,

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:01.890
the difference between
what the Democrat got

00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:04.145
and what Biden got two years ago,

00:25:04.145 --> 00:25:06.870
limiting it to the 150 Closest Race,

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:11.870
just so it's comparable across
regions and time periods.

00:25:12.030 --> 00:25:14.930
And because that's 60/40 and beyond that,

00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:16.770
it really doesn't matter much.

00:25:16.770 --> 00:25:21.570
Right, so the swing in
those 150 districts in 2018

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:23.805
was seven points away from Republicans

00:25:23.805 --> 00:25:26.040
and four points away from Democrats.

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:29.011
And that's the mental
model that people have.

00:25:29.011 --> 00:25:33.330
But if you split the
country into one country,

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:37.380
which is the what I'm
calling the MAGA war zone,

00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:38.849
which are the, you know,

00:25:38.849 --> 00:25:42.000
the electoral college states,

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:43.994
places where things are really fierce,

00:25:43.994 --> 00:25:45.720
and the rest of the country,

00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:47.437
you see that really
there was no difference

00:25:47.437 --> 00:25:50.607
between the swing that Trump endured

00:25:50.607 --> 00:25:52.590
and the swing that Democrats endured

00:25:52.590 --> 00:25:54.450
in that part of the country.

00:25:54.450 --> 00:25:56.806
But in the MAGA war zone,

00:25:56.806 --> 00:26:00.870
Democrats did barely less well than Biden

00:26:00.870 --> 00:26:02.511
did two years ago.

00:26:02.511 --> 00:26:05.370
And because I would argue people, voters

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:08.640
in those states understood
this is playing for keeps,

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:10.713
real MAGA, no MAGA.

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:15.480
And the other way of sort of looking at it

00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:17.790
in terms of the modeling was that

00:26:17.790 --> 00:26:20.370
I did 538 Final Deluxe,

00:26:20.370 --> 00:26:23.192
which has, like, all the
things they can throw into it,

00:26:23.192 --> 00:26:26.559
and Democrats over-performed
the estimate for them

00:26:26.559 --> 00:26:30.151
by 4.4 points in this MAGA war zone.

00:26:30.151 --> 00:26:34.834
And they, 538, you know,
came as close to nailing

00:26:34.834 --> 00:26:37.533
the rest of the districts,
as you can imagine.

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:40.539
And so the last thing is just to say that

00:26:40.539 --> 00:26:44.662
this idea of missing voters, it's the,

00:26:44.662 --> 00:26:46.860
and I'm sure we'll come
back to some questions,

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:48.840
but the dynamic that I think,

00:26:48.840 --> 00:26:50.951
now there was clearly
switching and stuff like that,

00:26:50.951 --> 00:26:53.147
but the dynamic is that

00:26:53.147 --> 00:26:57.210
turnout went up among Democrats

00:26:57.210 --> 00:26:58.980
in that MAGA war zone,

00:26:58.980 --> 00:27:01.604
turnout among Republicans
was about the same

00:27:01.604 --> 00:27:04.927
in both areas.

00:27:04.927 --> 00:27:09.041
And one of the things
that this helps explain

00:27:09.041 --> 00:27:14.041
that the bad candidate,
all the other kinds of,

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:17.550
you know, independent voter
explanations doesn't explain,

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:21.570
is why if you have an
independent, for example,

00:27:21.570 --> 00:27:23.910
comes out and says, "Mastriano, oh my god.

00:27:23.910 --> 00:27:25.860
I can't vote for them."

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:30.860
Did that person who other
than that were postulating,

00:27:30.875 --> 00:27:33.477
would have been a thermostatic voter,

00:27:33.477 --> 00:27:37.260
give Democrats big down
ballot gains in the state?

00:27:37.260 --> 00:27:39.381
How did they flip the two chambers

00:27:39.381 --> 00:27:41.970
in Michigan, right?

00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:45.151
It is because the big driver

00:27:45.151 --> 00:27:47.640
was that there were just more Democrats,

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:49.980
and it didn't matter
whether the Republican

00:27:49.980 --> 00:27:51.270
was a denier or not,

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:53.268
just having decided to come out and vote

00:27:53.268 --> 00:27:55.140
was what mattered.

00:27:55.140 --> 00:27:58.233
And I'll stop there, because
that's my time. Sorry.

00:27:59.790 --> 00:28:01.410
- Thanks, Mike.

00:28:01.410 --> 00:28:06.000
I will kind of pick up a
whole bunch of those threads

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:09.559
and see if we can

00:28:09.559 --> 00:28:12.630
tell a little bit of a broader story

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:16.950
about what is happening
in American politics

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:18.367
at this moment in time

00:28:18.367 --> 00:28:22.110
and what it might mean for the future.

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:24.390
And so the stuff that
I'm gonna talk about here

00:28:24.390 --> 00:28:28.020
in the next five or six
minutes comes from a book

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:30.360
that I've just written
called "The Bitter End"

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:33.000
with my colleague at
UCLA, Chris Tausanovitch

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:35.378
and my longtime co-author John Sides,

00:28:35.378 --> 00:28:37.530
who is at Vanderbilt.

00:28:37.530 --> 00:28:40.260
And our answer to this question
about what just happened

00:28:40.260 --> 00:28:43.903
in the midterms and what is
happening in American politics

00:28:43.903 --> 00:28:48.903
more generally, turns on
this idea of calcification.

00:28:49.620 --> 00:28:53.631
And calcification sounds
a lot like polarization,

00:28:53.631 --> 00:28:57.197
but it is not just polarization.

00:28:57.197 --> 00:29:00.870
I like to say it's polarization plus.

00:29:00.870 --> 00:29:04.710
And the plus is really important.

00:29:04.710 --> 00:29:07.876
So there are four
components of polarization,

00:29:07.876 --> 00:29:10.295
and I'll tell you what they are briefly.

00:29:10.295 --> 00:29:13.665
Two of them are a long time in the making,

00:29:13.665 --> 00:29:17.160
and two of them are more recent.

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:20.654
So the first two are sort
of long-term tectonic shifts

00:29:20.654 --> 00:29:22.200
that have taken time.

00:29:22.200 --> 00:29:24.547
And the last two you can think
of as little earthquakes,

00:29:24.547 --> 00:29:27.480
shocks, that have happened recently.

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:30.360
The first two sound a
lot like polarization.

00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:32.638
So an increasing homogeneity

00:29:32.638 --> 00:29:35.160
within each of the parties.

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:36.870
So people who call themselves Democrats

00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:38.874
are more alike than they've ever been

00:29:38.874 --> 00:29:42.960
on issues, on ideology, possibly
even on characteristics.

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:44.566
Same thing for Republicans.

00:29:44.566 --> 00:29:47.532
The second thing, is
an increasing distance

00:29:47.532 --> 00:29:49.800
between the two parties.

00:29:49.800 --> 00:29:52.997
Something that we've seen happen
in Congress for a long time

00:29:52.997 --> 00:29:56.310
but is now happening in the electorate.

00:29:56.310 --> 00:29:59.190
On average, people's
attitudes about policies

00:29:59.190 --> 00:30:00.942
in the electorate are very far apart

00:30:00.942 --> 00:30:03.933
when you sort them by political party.

00:30:04.950 --> 00:30:07.514
The more recent things include a change

00:30:07.514 --> 00:30:10.590
in the set of issues
that we're fighting over.

00:30:10.590 --> 00:30:13.677
So the importance of
identity-inflected issues,

00:30:13.677 --> 00:30:18.677
things that turn on race,
ethnicity, religion, gender.

00:30:18.706 --> 00:30:21.268
So that would be things
like abortion, immigration,

00:30:21.268 --> 00:30:24.600
the Muslim ban, separating
children at the border.

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:27.261
All of those kinds of person-based issues

00:30:27.261 --> 00:30:32.190
have become the thing that
is contested politically,

00:30:32.190 --> 00:30:35.670
as opposed to the New Deal type

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:37.440
of political contestation

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:41.340
that defined most of
politics in our lifetimes.

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:44.700
So the role and size of
government, the tax rate,

00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:47.880
whether small businesses should
be taxed higher or lower,

00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:50.473
whether low income families
should be taxed higher or lower.

00:30:50.473 --> 00:30:53.460
Joe the plumber is the classic example.

00:30:53.460 --> 00:30:55.320
If everybody remembers him

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:58.036
from the 2008 presidential campaign.

00:30:58.036 --> 00:30:59.790
We don't fight, we're not fighting

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:01.290
over those things anymore.

00:31:01.290 --> 00:31:03.665
So the dimension of conflict has shifted,

00:31:03.665 --> 00:31:07.080
and that's something that
has taken about a decade.

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:08.670
That's the more recent.

00:31:08.670 --> 00:31:12.360
The first two things are
multiple decades since the '60s,

00:31:12.360 --> 00:31:14.280
the Civil Rights and Voting Rights Act.

00:31:14.280 --> 00:31:17.430
These last two things are
within the last decade.

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:20.000
The final thing is partisan parity

00:31:20.000 --> 00:31:21.750
within the electorate.

00:31:21.750 --> 00:31:23.040
So a rough balance

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:24.879
between people calling
themselves Democrats

00:31:24.879 --> 00:31:27.403
and people calling themselves
Republicans right now

00:31:27.403 --> 00:31:28.260
in the electorate.

00:31:28.260 --> 00:31:29.790
It's not a perfect balance,

00:31:29.790 --> 00:31:34.311
but it's closer than it's been
in the last 70 or 80 years.

00:31:34.311 --> 00:31:36.837
Those four things mashed up together

00:31:36.837 --> 00:31:39.990
lead us to this state of politics

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:42.345
that we call calcification.

00:31:42.345 --> 00:31:44.287
And just like in the human body,

00:31:44.287 --> 00:31:48.351
calcification makes
things stiff and rigid.

00:31:48.351 --> 00:31:49.800
Why?

00:31:49.800 --> 00:31:53.852
Because the stakes of winning
elections are very high.

00:31:53.852 --> 00:31:55.230
Why?

00:31:55.230 --> 00:31:59.279
Because the other side is
farther away than ever.

00:31:59.279 --> 00:32:02.880
And because of the partisan parity,

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:06.474
victory is always within
reach for both sides.

00:32:06.474 --> 00:32:10.471
And we're fighting over
these extremely divisive,

00:32:10.471 --> 00:32:12.927
person-based issues.

00:32:12.927 --> 00:32:15.720
And so when you mash all
those things up together,

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:18.281
it becomes very difficult for voters,

00:32:18.281 --> 00:32:21.510
say whose party nominated a candidate

00:32:21.510 --> 00:32:23.700
who they don't think are high quality,

00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:26.737
becomes very difficult
for those voters to say,

00:32:26.737 --> 00:32:29.410
"Oh, well, okay, in
this particular election

00:32:29.410 --> 00:32:31.650
I'm gonna cross over and
vote for the other side,

00:32:31.650 --> 00:32:34.077
'cause I don't love the
person my party nominated."

00:32:34.077 --> 00:32:36.957
We're talking about a couple
of points on the tax rate

00:32:36.957 --> 00:32:39.960
or, you know, whether government

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:41.520
should really be involved in healthcare.

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:43.229
And maybe you can cross over.

00:32:43.229 --> 00:32:44.887
But when you're saying,

00:32:44.887 --> 00:32:46.890
"I don't think we should
be separating children

00:32:46.890 --> 00:32:48.517
from their families at the border."

00:32:48.517 --> 00:32:50.322
"I don't love the person
my party's nominated,

00:32:50.322 --> 00:32:52.710
but, you know what, I'm
gonna vote for the person

00:32:52.710 --> 00:32:54.031
who wants to separate the children,"

00:32:54.031 --> 00:32:58.020
it's a completely different
kind of stretching

00:32:58.020 --> 00:33:01.500
of your party affiliation
to vote for the other side.

00:33:01.500 --> 00:33:04.637
And so this context has produced

00:33:04.637 --> 00:33:08.940
this state of stickiness
that we call calcification.

00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:10.830
So I wanna show you just a couple

00:33:10.830 --> 00:33:13.830
of the consequences of calcification.

00:33:13.830 --> 00:33:18.150
The first is that voters have
observed these differences

00:33:18.150 --> 00:33:20.820
and appreciate them between the parties.

00:33:20.820 --> 00:33:22.503
Very different worlds on offer

00:33:22.503 --> 00:33:25.054
on these identity-inflected things

00:33:25.054 --> 00:33:28.993
between Republicans and
Democrats, and voters know it.

00:33:28.993 --> 00:33:32.406
So here I'm showing you
time on the horizontal axis,

00:33:32.406 --> 00:33:34.857
and the share of people
on the vertical axis

00:33:34.857 --> 00:33:37.860
who say they have seen
important differences

00:33:37.860 --> 00:33:40.290
between the two political parties.

00:33:40.290 --> 00:33:42.149
In 1952, you can see

00:33:42.149 --> 00:33:44.552
for reasons that everybody can figure out,

00:33:44.552 --> 00:33:47.070
only one out of two Americans thinks

00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:49.838
there are important differences
between the two parties.

00:33:49.838 --> 00:33:53.610
But today, that number is 90%.

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:56.285
So nine out of 10 Americans
think the two parties

00:33:56.285 --> 00:33:58.890
have important differences.

00:33:58.890 --> 00:34:00.180
So the first piece of evidence

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:04.308
that the state of politics
today is different for voters

00:34:04.308 --> 00:34:07.680
than it has been at any previous point

00:34:07.680 --> 00:34:10.020
over the last several decades.

00:34:10.020 --> 00:34:12.840
Here's another implication
of calcification.

00:34:12.840 --> 00:34:16.320
The emotional reaction
to political parties.

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:18.487
Here we're asking people
to rate their party

00:34:18.487 --> 00:34:21.930
and the other party on a zero to 100 scale

00:34:21.930 --> 00:34:23.400
that's like a thermometer.

00:34:23.400 --> 00:34:25.613
100 is very cold, you don't like them.

00:34:25.613 --> 00:34:28.200
Sorry, zero is very cold,
you don't like them.

00:34:28.200 --> 00:34:30.240
100 is hot, you do like them.

00:34:30.240 --> 00:34:32.730
50 is neutral, take it or leave it.

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:35.250
So people have always
liked their own party.

00:34:35.250 --> 00:34:37.290
Putting it up here in the 19,

00:34:37.290 --> 00:34:40.100
or sorry, in the 70 rating range

00:34:40.100 --> 00:34:43.351
from 1978 to the present day.

00:34:43.351 --> 00:34:46.273
But they like the
opposite party a lot less

00:34:46.273 --> 00:34:48.030
than they used to.

00:34:48.030 --> 00:34:50.477
We used to have neutral
feelings about the out party.

00:34:50.477 --> 00:34:54.090
And now you can see we're down here at 19.

00:34:54.090 --> 00:34:56.790
People really dislike the opposite party.

00:34:56.790 --> 00:34:59.993
That emotional reaction can
be based on a lot of things.

00:34:59.993 --> 00:35:02.543
But certainly a component

00:35:02.543 --> 00:35:05.700
is the figure I showed
you just a minute ago,

00:35:05.700 --> 00:35:07.714
that people perceive important differences

00:35:07.714 --> 00:35:08.877
in the kind of world

00:35:08.877 --> 00:35:11.490
that each of these parties wanna create.

00:35:11.490 --> 00:35:12.810
And they don't like the world

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:15.093
that the out party wants to create.

00:35:15.943 --> 00:35:18.330
Another consequence of calcification

00:35:18.330 --> 00:35:19.650
is something that you've heard a couple

00:35:19.650 --> 00:35:21.236
of the other panelists talk about,

00:35:21.236 --> 00:35:24.690
that elections are becoming tighter.

00:35:24.690 --> 00:35:26.430
There's less swing.

00:35:26.430 --> 00:35:29.340
And so very quickly, I'm
just showing you here

00:35:29.340 --> 00:35:31.509
the two-party vote share for the Democrats

00:35:31.509 --> 00:35:36.030
between '08 and '12, between '12 and '16,

00:35:36.030 --> 00:35:38.151
and then between '16 and '20.

00:35:38.151 --> 00:35:40.639
Each dot is a state.

00:35:40.639 --> 00:35:43.620
And this diagonal 45 degree line

00:35:43.620 --> 00:35:46.020
would be where the dots would all be

00:35:46.020 --> 00:35:48.215
if the Democratic party got
the same share of the vote

00:35:48.215 --> 00:35:52.950
in the '08 election, for
example, and the '12 election.

00:35:52.950 --> 00:35:55.444
So you can quickly see the
dots are not on the line.

00:35:55.444 --> 00:35:58.578
Between '08 and '12, the
dots are below the line

00:35:58.578 --> 00:36:00.660
indicating that in every state,

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:03.272
Obama did worse in '12 than he did in '08.

00:36:03.272 --> 00:36:04.662
Easy to understand.

00:36:04.662 --> 00:36:07.470
He gets a big boost
and wins in a landslide

00:36:07.470 --> 00:36:09.254
because of the global financial crisis.

00:36:09.254 --> 00:36:12.812
And then in 2012, some
of those voters peel off.

00:36:12.812 --> 00:36:14.773
This one's a little more complicated.

00:36:14.773 --> 00:36:18.120
Hillary Clinton picking
up votes relative to Obama

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:20.788
in states like California,
Arizona, and Texas.

00:36:20.788 --> 00:36:25.788
But losing them in states like
Ohio, Pennsylvania and Iowa.

00:36:25.789 --> 00:36:28.283
This is a reshuffling of the states

00:36:28.283 --> 00:36:32.640
into a new equilibrium
that you can see persisting

00:36:32.640 --> 00:36:35.520
between 2016 and 2020.

00:36:35.520 --> 00:36:36.660
How do you see that?

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:39.353
Because all of these dots
are very close to the line

00:36:39.353 --> 00:36:41.527
and they are almost on the line.

00:36:41.527 --> 00:36:45.900
This tells us that the 2020
election was almost a replay

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:48.630
of the 2016 presidential election.

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:51.480
Very few people, there could be, you know,

00:36:51.480 --> 00:36:52.620
Mike just talked about turnout.

00:36:52.620 --> 00:36:54.180
That's really important to hear.

00:36:54.180 --> 00:36:55.170
There could be different people

00:36:55.170 --> 00:36:56.522
turning out in these elections,

00:36:56.522 --> 00:37:00.150
but the outcome of these
elections state by state

00:37:00.150 --> 00:37:03.960
is very close '16 to '20.

00:37:03.960 --> 00:37:06.379
You can see it at the
county level as well.

00:37:06.379 --> 00:37:09.120
Here we're showing you over time

00:37:09.120 --> 00:37:13.278
the average county level
swing from the last election

00:37:13.278 --> 00:37:17.790
to the one that is indicated
on the horizontal axis.

00:37:17.790 --> 00:37:20.220
Some of the swings have been very big.

00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:22.860
20 point average county swings

00:37:22.860 --> 00:37:26.837
in presidential election
outcomes in the '60s and '70s.

00:37:26.837 --> 00:37:30.930
But you can see over time, this
county swing is going down.

00:37:30.930 --> 00:37:34.200
In 2020, it's the smallest
value it has ever been.

00:37:34.200 --> 00:37:36.283
It's about three percentage points.

00:37:36.283 --> 00:37:38.113
That's calcification.

00:37:38.113 --> 00:37:40.696
Everything is more the same

00:37:40.696 --> 00:37:43.415
and looks a lot like the last time

00:37:43.415 --> 00:37:46.209
relative to what we've seen in the past.

00:37:46.209 --> 00:37:47.795
And then finally, just to wrap up,

00:37:47.795 --> 00:37:49.963
what about what just happened?

00:37:49.963 --> 00:37:52.530
Here's the Georgia runoff.

00:37:52.530 --> 00:37:53.363
Ooh, sorry.

00:37:53.363 --> 00:37:57.060
Didn't mean to to hit that button.

00:37:57.060 --> 00:38:01.444
Okay, here's 2021 on the
left and 2022 on the right.

00:38:01.444 --> 00:38:05.360
And if you just stare
at this and look at it,

00:38:05.360 --> 00:38:09.275
you will be able to see
that they're very similar.

00:38:09.275 --> 00:38:11.700
A lot of red at the top in both places.

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:15.516
Blue around the urban areas
and the college campuses.

00:38:15.516 --> 00:38:18.690
Almost everywhere except
for the right hand part

00:38:18.690 --> 00:38:20.820
of the state here,

00:38:20.820 --> 00:38:23.876
which became a little redder in 2022.

00:38:23.876 --> 00:38:25.410
It looks very similar.

00:38:25.410 --> 00:38:28.082
That's calcification
in this runoff election

00:38:28.082 --> 00:38:30.450
just in Georgia.

00:38:30.450 --> 00:38:33.390
And then finally, another sense of, like,

00:38:33.390 --> 00:38:35.590
the difference between the two parties,

00:38:35.590 --> 00:38:38.283
it's just to look at a couple counties.

00:38:38.283 --> 00:38:43.283
So here's a red county just
outside of Atlanta, okay?

00:38:44.070 --> 00:38:48.570
And 70/30 roughly, for Walker.

00:38:48.570 --> 00:38:52.365
An adjacent county
right here near Atlanta,

00:38:52.365 --> 00:38:55.620
80/20 for Warnock.

00:38:55.620 --> 00:38:58.221
These are people living
in the suburbs of Atlanta.

00:38:58.221 --> 00:39:01.200
They're roughly living
the same kind of lives,

00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:02.870
but they're living in
very different places

00:39:02.870 --> 00:39:05.940
and they know it and they appreciate it.

00:39:05.940 --> 00:39:10.772
And all of this is leading
to this sense of being stuck.

00:39:10.772 --> 00:39:14.160
Don't want the world that
the other side is offering.

00:39:14.160 --> 00:39:15.783
And the idea that calcification,

00:39:15.783 --> 00:39:18.613
because the stakes of
elections are so high,

00:39:18.613 --> 00:39:20.703
the other side is far away,

00:39:21.600 --> 00:39:24.360
has left us at a point where
very little is gonna change.

00:39:24.360 --> 00:39:26.907
These elections are gonna
be very close going forward.

00:39:26.907 --> 00:39:29.340
And that means that literally anything

00:39:29.340 --> 00:39:30.570
could be flipping them.

00:39:30.570 --> 00:39:33.480
Calcification doesn't mean
the same side always wins,

00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:36.120
it means the elections
are gonna be razor thin.

00:39:36.120 --> 00:39:37.681
All those things Mike just talked about,

00:39:37.681 --> 00:39:40.231
all the things that Josh
and Mo talked about,

00:39:40.231 --> 00:39:43.980
are going to be swinging these elections,

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:48.120
and the era of the
blowout is probably gone.

00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:50.203
How long is it gonna last?

00:39:50.203 --> 00:39:53.910
My guess is probably
the rest of my lifetime.

00:39:53.910 --> 00:39:55.842
But a new dimension of
conflict is what we need

00:39:55.842 --> 00:40:00.480
in order to shift us out
of this current state.

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:02.280
And we could spend a lot of time

00:40:02.280 --> 00:40:04.593
thinking about exactly what that is.

00:40:05.430 --> 00:40:06.630
And I'll end there.

00:40:06.630 --> 00:40:08.280
And I think we're all gonna come back on.

00:40:08.280 --> 00:40:10.050
Is that right, Alan?

00:40:10.050 --> 00:40:11.360
- Yes. Yes, it is, Lynn.

00:40:11.360 --> 00:40:12.561
Yes, it is.

00:40:12.561 --> 00:40:14.310
Okay, terrific.

00:40:14.310 --> 00:40:16.820
So let's take a few seconds

00:40:16.820 --> 00:40:19.203
for the panel to reassemble.

00:40:21.060 --> 00:40:22.050
While we're doing that,

00:40:22.050 --> 00:40:26.759
let me again urge our audience members

00:40:26.759 --> 00:40:31.650
to formulate and share their questions

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:34.310
in the Q&amp;A.

00:40:34.310 --> 00:40:36.987
There are some questions now and we're,

00:40:36.987 --> 00:40:40.360
I'd be very interested in
knowing what your questions are

00:40:42.021 --> 00:40:44.520
so the panelists can
address those questions.

00:40:44.520 --> 00:40:47.621
So let me just start by
asking the panelists,

00:40:47.621 --> 00:40:49.856
these were four, I think very interesting

00:40:49.856 --> 00:40:54.856
and fairly different presentations.

00:40:56.342 --> 00:40:59.550
One of the great things about a panel is

00:40:59.550 --> 00:41:04.550
you get to actually react and
talk to your fellow panelists.

00:41:04.560 --> 00:41:06.540
Does anyone just wanna start with,

00:41:06.540 --> 00:41:09.090
and I hope we'll be able to
do this for a few minutes.

00:41:09.090 --> 00:41:12.390
Let's see if this actually works.

00:41:12.390 --> 00:41:15.150
Do you have a question you'd like to ask

00:41:15.150 --> 00:41:16.900
or comment you'd like to make

00:41:18.510 --> 00:41:20.130
about something that

00:41:20.130 --> 00:41:22.000
one of your fellow panelists

00:41:25.110 --> 00:41:27.994
presented on, either one
of their observations

00:41:27.994 --> 00:41:30.900
or conclusions or something like that?

00:41:30.900 --> 00:41:31.733
And...

00:41:35.280 --> 00:41:37.713
I see great eagerness among our panel.

00:41:38.885 --> 00:41:40.454
- I'll ask.

00:41:40.454 --> 00:41:42.780
- Lynn, please just go right ahead.

00:41:42.780 --> 00:41:46.143
- Okay, so. Hi, Mo.

00:41:47.067 --> 00:41:50.760
You mentioned the swing voters,

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:53.472
and that we've always known
that campaigns were playing

00:41:53.472 --> 00:41:56.310
to these people who were
the swing in the middle.

00:41:56.310 --> 00:41:59.554
And I wondered if you thought that

00:41:59.554 --> 00:42:04.350
that share of the electorate
has gotten smaller.

00:42:04.350 --> 00:42:08.010
So obviously the margin
is still really important,

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:10.320
but, you know, maybe if we
used to have swing voters

00:42:10.320 --> 00:42:12.750
that were this much, now it's this much.

00:42:12.750 --> 00:42:14.700
I wonder, can you say a little bit

00:42:14.700 --> 00:42:16.050
about how you think of that?

00:42:16.050 --> 00:42:17.640
- Sure. Am I on?

00:42:17.640 --> 00:42:19.263
Okay, yeah.

00:42:20.190 --> 00:42:22.680
Yeah, I think it has gotten smaller,

00:42:22.680 --> 00:42:23.970
but no less important.

00:42:23.970 --> 00:42:27.090
In fact, more important that
what you talked about earlier,

00:42:27.090 --> 00:42:28.800
the parity of the parties.

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:32.479
It doesn't matter if
the swing voters are 2%,

00:42:32.479 --> 00:42:34.918
if the parties are divided 49/49,

00:42:34.918 --> 00:42:36.900
they still determine the election.

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:38.790
So essentially, you know, it doesn't,

00:42:38.790 --> 00:42:41.520
they don't have to be a huge
quantity to decide an election.

00:42:41.520 --> 00:42:44.670
Now, I do take issue with this belief

00:42:44.670 --> 00:42:47.113
among journalists and among
younger political scientists

00:42:47.113 --> 00:42:49.278
that nearly all leaning independents

00:42:49.278 --> 00:42:51.291
are in fact closet partisans.

00:42:51.291 --> 00:42:55.530
And I have evidence against
that in my last book,

00:42:55.530 --> 00:42:57.300
but nobody seems to
have read that chapter.

00:42:57.300 --> 00:42:58.350
I can't remember.

00:42:58.350 --> 00:43:01.050
But anyway, because they switch
from election to election,

00:43:01.050 --> 00:43:03.090
the same person who says,
"I'm leaning independent"

00:43:03.090 --> 00:43:06.090
in one election tells you,
"I'm leaning Democrat"

00:43:06.090 --> 00:43:07.520
in one election, then tells
you, "I'm leaning Republican"

00:43:07.520 --> 00:43:08.820
in the next election.

00:43:08.820 --> 00:43:10.320
So their party idea, their leaning,

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:13.050
is following their vote
decision rather than vice versa.

00:43:13.050 --> 00:43:17.139
But yeah, I think in an era
of closed party competition,

00:43:17.139 --> 00:43:20.079
the independents whatever
their size, the middle ground,

00:43:20.079 --> 00:43:21.690
I mean, and by the way,

00:43:21.690 --> 00:43:24.210
independents are not all
principled centrists.

00:43:24.210 --> 00:43:25.982
They're just, they're moderates.

00:43:25.982 --> 00:43:28.800
They are people who are
disengaged from politics.

00:43:28.800 --> 00:43:29.943
They are cross pressured people

00:43:29.943 --> 00:43:31.680
who don't really like either party.

00:43:31.680 --> 00:43:33.870
They're clueless people, you know.

00:43:33.870 --> 00:43:35.040
They're this, but they,

00:43:35.040 --> 00:43:37.590
the thing they have in
common is they're volatile.

00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:39.930
That particular campaign and candidates

00:43:39.930 --> 00:43:42.570
can make them change their
votes from election to election.

00:43:42.570 --> 00:43:45.270
So they're in the driver's
seat basically in this era.

00:43:49.500 --> 00:43:53.310
- Yeah, if I can jump in
on the independent point

00:43:53.310 --> 00:43:54.143
for a minute,

00:43:54.143 --> 00:43:57.660
and get something I
sort of tried to wave at

00:43:57.660 --> 00:43:59.280
in what I was saying,

00:43:59.280 --> 00:44:02.000
is that I think that,
some of you know this,

00:44:02.000 --> 00:44:06.360
that we really limited our imagination

00:44:06.360 --> 00:44:11.360
of how politics happens to
what we can see in polls.

00:44:11.640 --> 00:44:15.840
And so on the independent point, right?

00:44:15.840 --> 00:44:18.930
I think one of the things
we saw this time around,

00:44:18.930 --> 00:44:21.300
and part of what I was trying
to get at in the difference

00:44:21.300 --> 00:44:25.080
between living in a MAGA
war zone state and not,

00:44:25.080 --> 00:44:27.295
is that the people around you

00:44:27.295 --> 00:44:29.100
have a very different feeling

00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:32.280
about what's at stake
in the election, right?

00:44:32.280 --> 00:44:34.069
And so if you were in Georgia,

00:44:34.069 --> 00:44:36.755
you understood that even if
you thought Herschel Walker

00:44:36.755 --> 00:44:39.000
was the biggest idiot in the world,

00:44:39.000 --> 00:44:42.240
that you didn't want Chuck Schumer

00:44:42.240 --> 00:44:43.793
to be majority leader,

00:44:43.793 --> 00:44:46.740
and you knew exactly
what you were voting on.

00:44:46.740 --> 00:44:48.450
It wasn't about issues,

00:44:48.450 --> 00:44:51.024
it wasn't about any of those things.

00:44:51.024 --> 00:44:52.290
On the other hand,

00:44:52.290 --> 00:44:55.342
I am as convinced as you
can be about this stuff,

00:44:55.342 --> 00:44:58.391
that there were a lot of
folks who voted for Biden

00:44:58.391 --> 00:45:01.755
in California and New York
who did not understand

00:45:01.755 --> 00:45:05.048
that by staying home they were making

00:45:05.048 --> 00:45:07.500
Kevin McCarthy speaker.

00:45:07.500 --> 00:45:09.630
Because no one was talking about it.

00:45:09.630 --> 00:45:12.971
No one was saying that there
was this national dimension

00:45:12.971 --> 00:45:16.554
to the election in that
kind of hot house way

00:45:16.554 --> 00:45:19.020
it was in the other states.

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:21.903
And you're just not gonna
see that on a poll, right?

00:45:22.890 --> 00:45:24.690
But that is actually how the world works.

00:45:24.690 --> 00:45:26.268
I mean, that's how, you know,

00:45:26.268 --> 00:45:28.830
like what you see in your social network,

00:45:28.830 --> 00:45:30.889
it's what people are talking about, right?

00:45:30.889 --> 00:45:35.100
And so I think that that's
another part of the story

00:45:35.100 --> 00:45:37.916
that gets left out on independents, right?

00:45:37.916 --> 00:45:39.780
I think what you were saying is right.

00:45:39.780 --> 00:45:40.613
They're the clueless people, right?

00:45:40.613 --> 00:45:42.180
I mean, if you're living in this country

00:45:42.180 --> 00:45:44.250
and don't understand what's up, right?

00:45:44.250 --> 00:45:46.309
Then you're gonna swing back and forth.

00:45:46.309 --> 00:45:49.440
But if you're in that,
like, Madison or, like,

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:51.900
in Wisconsin and you're, you know,

00:45:51.900 --> 00:45:53.910
you go to a diner for
breakfast every morning,

00:45:53.910 --> 00:45:55.503
you're sitting at the
big table in the middle,

00:45:55.503 --> 00:45:58.860
you know who you're
supposed to vote for, right?

00:45:58.860 --> 00:46:01.113
And if you're in a different place

00:46:01.113 --> 00:46:04.079
where it isn't, like, acute politics,

00:46:04.079 --> 00:46:07.230
you don't, and so you're,
like, wagging back and forth.

00:46:07.230 --> 00:46:09.180
And I think there are
lots of things like that

00:46:09.180 --> 00:46:11.964
that we don't take into
the analysis and story

00:46:11.964 --> 00:46:15.990
about why we get the results we do.

00:46:15.990 --> 00:46:18.711
- If I can follow up on with Mike,

00:46:18.711 --> 00:46:20.280
so there's all this talk

00:46:20.280 --> 00:46:22.590
of the nationalization
of American politics

00:46:22.590 --> 00:46:25.050
with the demise of local
media and people relying

00:46:25.050 --> 00:46:27.926
more on national sources with people,

00:46:27.926 --> 00:46:30.425
you know, tuning into
national social media

00:46:30.425 --> 00:46:32.130
rather than a local sense,

00:46:32.130 --> 00:46:34.029
people knowing less about their national,

00:46:34.029 --> 00:46:35.550
their local candidates even,

00:46:35.550 --> 00:46:37.080
as they know more about the differences

00:46:37.080 --> 00:46:40.170
between the national
parties, as Lynn was sharing.

00:46:40.170 --> 00:46:41.340
If it's true that there's this

00:46:41.340 --> 00:46:43.247
nationalization of American politics,

00:46:43.247 --> 00:46:45.483
why does it matter if
you're an independent voter

00:46:45.483 --> 00:46:49.680
in California or an
independent voter in Georgia?

00:46:49.680 --> 00:46:52.710
Or do you think we as political scientists

00:46:52.710 --> 00:46:54.060
might be overstating the case

00:46:54.060 --> 00:46:56.310
of this nationalization
of American politics?

00:46:57.660 --> 00:47:00.570
- I think that what I should say is that

00:47:00.570 --> 00:47:02.723
it's not...

00:47:02.723 --> 00:47:07.723
It's sensitive to the context

00:47:07.920 --> 00:47:09.660
in the moment, right?

00:47:09.660 --> 00:47:14.040
That during the, you know,

00:47:14.040 --> 00:47:16.560
Trump administration years, right?

00:47:16.560 --> 00:47:19.170
You could not hide anywhere

00:47:19.170 --> 00:47:21.550
and not hear about all this stuff, right?

00:47:21.550 --> 00:47:24.091
And in the last two years,

00:47:24.091 --> 00:47:26.490
if you're not living
in one of these states

00:47:26.490 --> 00:47:29.280
where they're doing scam
audits or, you know,

00:47:29.280 --> 00:47:32.031
doing lots of high visibility things,

00:47:32.031 --> 00:47:35.400
you can, and if you're
not really clued in,

00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:37.791
then you kind of think things
have gone back to normal.

00:47:37.791 --> 00:47:41.160
And so I don't think you
can say there's, like,

00:47:41.160 --> 00:47:43.638
one way to understand independent voters

00:47:43.638 --> 00:47:46.533
in every state at all times, right?

00:47:48.030 --> 00:47:50.550
- So in some sense, Biden being boring

00:47:50.550 --> 00:47:53.760
has brought back this less
of a national dimension

00:47:53.760 --> 00:47:54.593
that existed under Trump.

00:47:54.593 --> 00:47:56.910
- Yeah, no, I think that
that's been a big problem

00:47:56.910 --> 00:47:58.560
for the first year and a half,

00:47:58.560 --> 00:48:03.503
was that he was really going
against the only motivation

00:48:03.503 --> 00:48:06.270
that would get people to do

00:48:06.270 --> 00:48:07.593
what they ended up doing.

00:48:10.320 --> 00:48:11.430
- Good. Okay.

00:48:11.430 --> 00:48:14.520
Any other follow ups on
that line of discussion?

00:48:14.520 --> 00:48:17.937
One question I was wondering
about as this discussion

00:48:17.937 --> 00:48:19.170
was going on was,

00:48:19.170 --> 00:48:21.875
is there an implication
of the calcification ideas

00:48:21.875 --> 00:48:26.875
or that candidate quality
would be less important?

00:48:28.020 --> 00:48:28.853
And if so,

00:48:28.853 --> 00:48:31.980
is that what you're
predicting that we'll see,

00:48:31.980 --> 00:48:34.222
'cause it did seem like
some of the arguments

00:48:34.222 --> 00:48:36.960
Josh and I think Mo
might have made as well,

00:48:36.960 --> 00:48:40.140
was that, and I think
it's anecdotal evidence,

00:48:40.140 --> 00:48:42.470
I haven't seen, I've seen
a little bit of analysis,

00:48:42.470 --> 00:48:47.390
was that the, you know,
"candidate quality" problem

00:48:47.390 --> 00:48:51.233
really did manifest itself.

00:48:51.233 --> 00:48:53.580
And you know, you can
see it in the difference

00:48:53.580 --> 00:48:55.826
between up and down ballot voting

00:48:55.826 --> 00:49:00.826
and some of these cases
among other indicators.

00:49:01.380 --> 00:49:02.213
- For sure.

00:49:02.213 --> 00:49:04.590
Ohio and Pennsylvania are
great examples of that.

00:49:04.590 --> 00:49:05.955
- Georgia.

00:49:05.955 --> 00:49:08.973
- And yeah, I would say that,

00:49:10.384 --> 00:49:12.090
you know, I'm never gonna say

00:49:12.090 --> 00:49:14.970
that candidate quality
is irrelevant. (laughs)

00:49:14.970 --> 00:49:17.400
- That's true. (chuckles)

00:49:17.400 --> 00:49:19.950
- And I think that

00:49:19.950 --> 00:49:21.520
what we're saying is that

00:49:22.795 --> 00:49:25.290
take the argument that
Mo made a minute ago

00:49:25.290 --> 00:49:28.172
about those swing voters
being critically important now

00:49:28.172 --> 00:49:31.966
and, you know, what's swinging them.

00:49:31.966 --> 00:49:34.470
I think of these swing voters as being,

00:49:34.470 --> 00:49:35.610
they're trying to speak a language

00:49:35.610 --> 00:49:37.500
and their dictionary's a little broken.

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:40.290
Like, they're trying to
participate in politics,

00:49:40.290 --> 00:49:42.030
but, like, they just,
they don't pay attention,

00:49:42.030 --> 00:49:42.870
they don't watch the news,

00:49:42.870 --> 00:49:44.010
they don't understand the issues,

00:49:44.010 --> 00:49:45.412
they don't really care about very much.

00:49:45.412 --> 00:49:47.220
And so, and that is,

00:49:47.220 --> 00:49:49.800
I know that that really
sounds, like, disparaging

00:49:49.800 --> 00:49:51.150
and I didn't mean it to sound that way.

00:49:51.150 --> 00:49:53.610
But they're trying to communicate.

00:49:53.610 --> 00:49:54.810
Their dictionary's a little broken.

00:49:54.810 --> 00:49:56.850
So things like candidate quality,

00:49:56.850 --> 00:49:59.640
that really means a lot,

00:49:59.640 --> 00:50:01.708
'cause it's easy to
understand, it's accessible.

00:50:01.708 --> 00:50:05.280
And so I think that we wouldn't say

00:50:05.280 --> 00:50:08.430
that it's, you know, it may
be affecting fewer people

00:50:08.430 --> 00:50:09.480
than it has in the past,

00:50:09.480 --> 00:50:11.430
but it may be pivotal as ever.

00:50:11.430 --> 00:50:12.810
- Yeah, actually I'm gonna jump

00:50:12.810 --> 00:50:14.197
on the candidate quality thing,

00:50:14.197 --> 00:50:18.030
because I think this really
needs to be differentiated.

00:50:18.030 --> 00:50:21.000
Candidate quality absolutely matters

00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:24.155
for governor, right?

00:50:24.155 --> 00:50:27.077
Because it doesn't have
this national implication.

00:50:27.077 --> 00:50:30.047
That's how you get Hogan or Baker

00:50:30.047 --> 00:50:31.740
or something like that

00:50:31.740 --> 00:50:35.820
by people who are voting
70/30 Democratic nationally.

00:50:35.820 --> 00:50:38.172
So, you know, if you,

00:50:38.172 --> 00:50:40.830
I can't even remember the
guy in Maryland where I live,

00:50:40.830 --> 00:50:45.060
who is, like, the wacko
MAGA Republican, right?

00:50:45.060 --> 00:50:47.670
Who got about seven
votes this time, right?

00:50:47.670 --> 00:50:51.390
I mean, that's candidate quality
at the gubernatorial level.

00:50:51.390 --> 00:50:53.370
And so I think if we're
talking candidate quality,

00:50:53.370 --> 00:50:55.020
we really should just limit it.

00:50:55.020 --> 00:50:57.180
They are two different questions.

00:50:57.180 --> 00:50:58.440
The other thing, though,

00:50:58.440 --> 00:51:00.360
and this is where I think sometimes we,

00:51:00.360 --> 00:51:03.410
because we are, like, grounded
in polls and attitudes,

00:51:03.410 --> 00:51:07.080
rather than if you go to focus groups

00:51:07.080 --> 00:51:09.120
or talk to voters and all that,

00:51:09.120 --> 00:51:11.580
it's not that people just have

00:51:11.580 --> 00:51:13.848
different attitudes about policies,

00:51:13.848 --> 00:51:16.312
it's that we've evolved different ways

00:51:16.312 --> 00:51:18.510
of living in the country.

00:51:18.510 --> 00:51:20.898
And so it isn't just about, you know,

00:51:20.898 --> 00:51:23.850
is this person agreeing with me,

00:51:23.850 --> 00:51:27.450
but could I lose the right to an abortion?

00:51:27.450 --> 00:51:28.283
Right?

00:51:28.283 --> 00:51:30.140
Like, in my life, right?

00:51:30.140 --> 00:51:32.542
It's a different thing.

00:51:32.542 --> 00:51:37.000
And so I think Kari Lake
offers a really good

00:51:37.890 --> 00:51:41.541
counter example, because
if you turn the volume off

00:51:41.541 --> 00:51:43.188
and sorry, Katie,

00:51:43.188 --> 00:51:46.554
she was, like, one of the best candidates

00:51:46.554 --> 00:51:48.390
this time around.

00:51:48.390 --> 00:51:51.590
She had deep roots and
credibility as a news anchor.

00:51:51.590 --> 00:51:53.934
She came across well.

00:51:53.934 --> 00:51:57.060
It's just that people
understood what she was gonna do

00:51:57.060 --> 00:51:58.560
to the state, right?

00:51:58.560 --> 00:52:01.140
That's not, when we think
of bad candidate quality,

00:52:01.140 --> 00:52:06.036
we think of Sharron Angle or
Aiken or Mastriano, right?

00:52:06.036 --> 00:52:09.690
So I think it's, we have to parse out,

00:52:09.690 --> 00:52:11.820
that there's a substantive
dimension to this

00:52:11.820 --> 00:52:15.630
in this MAGA era that is different

00:52:15.630 --> 00:52:17.902
from their bad politicians.

00:52:17.902 --> 00:52:19.094
- Yeah, no, that's fair.

00:52:19.094 --> 00:52:21.330
Yeah, I think it was even the case,

00:52:21.330 --> 00:52:22.950
I haven't looked at this in quite a while,

00:52:22.950 --> 00:52:25.920
but back in the day I looked at candidates

00:52:25.920 --> 00:52:29.580
for Senate elections and
if I remember correctly,

00:52:29.580 --> 00:52:32.250
one of the fake outs, in a sense,

00:52:32.250 --> 00:52:37.146
was the governor of the wrong party

00:52:37.146 --> 00:52:38.520
who looked like they would be

00:52:38.520 --> 00:52:40.623
a very good candidate for Senate.

00:52:42.210 --> 00:52:45.120
And when they moved up,
tried to move up from Senate,

00:52:45.120 --> 00:52:47.050
from governor, they sometimes did well.

00:52:47.050 --> 00:52:49.110
Well, they were governor after all.

00:52:49.110 --> 00:52:52.530
But they were not the, they often lost.

00:52:52.530 --> 00:52:54.810
And they often weren't able to move

00:52:54.810 --> 00:52:57.390
from being the governor of the,

00:52:57.390 --> 00:52:59.670
so a governor of Massachusetts
would be an example,

00:52:59.670 --> 00:53:00.503
as you were saying.

00:53:00.503 --> 00:53:01.830
They were very good governors.

00:53:01.830 --> 00:53:03.388
They weren't gonna be
able to go to federal,

00:53:03.388 --> 00:53:07.110
equivalent level federal
office for the obvious reasons.

00:53:07.110 --> 00:53:08.231
And I imagine that might even be

00:53:08.231 --> 00:53:11.602
more of a problem this time around

00:53:11.602 --> 00:53:13.380
in this era.

00:53:13.380 --> 00:53:18.380
Okay, other questions or
comments by panelists?

00:53:19.230 --> 00:53:20.468
- Can I just add one more?

00:53:20.468 --> 00:53:21.363
- Please.

00:53:22.380 --> 00:53:24.270
- You know, the House incumbency advantage

00:53:24.270 --> 00:53:27.364
has declined steadily over,
like, the last 20 or 25 years.

00:53:27.364 --> 00:53:29.668
And I think it's directly
tied to the party differences

00:53:29.668 --> 00:53:30.998
we've talked about here,

00:53:30.998 --> 00:53:33.540
that in an era when
basically it doesn't matter

00:53:33.540 --> 00:53:34.710
which Republican you elect,

00:53:34.710 --> 00:53:36.991
it's gonna vote the same
way as any other Republican,

00:53:36.991 --> 00:53:39.243
and the committees are centralized

00:53:39.243 --> 00:53:42.300
and under the leadership
of the leadership.

00:53:42.300 --> 00:53:44.940
So it's simply a question of
do you want Mitch McConnell

00:53:44.940 --> 00:53:46.650
or Chuck Schumer in the House?

00:53:46.650 --> 00:53:50.250
I mean, do you want Nancy
Pelosi or Kevin McCarthy

00:53:50.250 --> 00:53:51.513
to be the speaker?

00:53:51.513 --> 00:53:53.623
And so I think that's why people

00:53:53.623 --> 00:53:55.701
are simply not paying
nearly as much attention

00:53:55.701 --> 00:53:57.264
to who their House incumbents are

00:53:57.264 --> 00:53:59.793
as they were 20, 25 years ago.

00:54:01.920 --> 00:54:03.633
- Other thoughts?

00:54:05.310 --> 00:54:07.013
Just one other question just because I,

00:54:07.013 --> 00:54:10.696
this is such an interesting
group of scholars

00:54:10.696 --> 00:54:13.923
and observers of American politics.

00:54:15.300 --> 00:54:16.980
And I think we'll probably end up going,

00:54:16.980 --> 00:54:18.300
when we go to the question and answer,

00:54:18.300 --> 00:54:22.620
right back to political
behavior and elections.

00:54:22.620 --> 00:54:25.050
Does anyone care to offer comments

00:54:25.050 --> 00:54:26.313
about implications

00:54:26.313 --> 00:54:30.663
of their assessments for governance?

00:54:31.530 --> 00:54:33.530
For what this means for...

00:54:34.410 --> 00:54:39.410
Or for what is the public policy output,

00:54:39.450 --> 00:54:41.490
the democratic, you know,

00:54:41.490 --> 00:54:44.523
the implications for
democracy, things like that.

00:54:45.480 --> 00:54:47.370
So what implications, what have you,

00:54:47.370 --> 00:54:48.870
what is your take away

00:54:48.870 --> 00:54:52.470
after this election regarding prospects?

00:54:52.470 --> 00:54:54.600
Or have you learned
anything from this election

00:54:54.600 --> 00:54:56.280
or do you have anything you wanna share

00:54:56.280 --> 00:54:58.650
regarding your views about governance

00:54:58.650 --> 00:55:01.833
or the state of our democracy?

00:55:04.110 --> 00:55:05.193
- It looks bad.

00:55:07.290 --> 00:55:09.511
- Yeah, would you care to elaborate?

00:55:09.511 --> 00:55:11.160
(guests laugh)
Dr. Fiorina?

00:55:11.160 --> 00:55:12.473
- Well, I mean,

00:55:12.473 --> 00:55:14.303
what is Congress gonna do?

00:55:14.303 --> 00:55:17.100
They're just revving up investigations,

00:55:17.100 --> 00:55:20.588
and we're sort of starting on
the 2024 campaign right now.

00:55:20.588 --> 00:55:23.940
And Republicans are gonna
obviously have a big fight

00:55:23.940 --> 00:55:26.130
between the MAGA types
who want to get back

00:55:26.130 --> 00:55:27.510
at every Democrat they can think about,

00:55:27.510 --> 00:55:30.229
and they're gonna be
conducting investigations,

00:55:30.229 --> 00:55:32.668
and a wing that would like
to show they might have some

00:55:32.668 --> 00:55:35.070
ability to govern should
they get a majority

00:55:35.070 --> 00:55:36.360
and should they win the presidency.

00:55:36.360 --> 00:55:39.390
And, you know, I just think the Democrats

00:55:39.390 --> 00:55:40.860
are gonna be frustrated,

00:55:40.860 --> 00:55:42.510
not being able to get anything done.

00:55:42.510 --> 00:55:45.240
So I think two years, nothing happens.

00:55:45.240 --> 00:55:46.443
Things just get worse.

00:55:47.502 --> 00:55:52.276
- I think it was a complete
disaster, catastrophe

00:55:52.276 --> 00:55:55.650
for governance and for, like,

00:55:55.650 --> 00:55:59.146
people will look back on this
as a really big loss moment.

00:55:59.146 --> 00:56:03.600
If you remember, like, after
the second impeachment trial,

00:56:03.600 --> 00:56:06.270
the sort of savvy opinion
and all the people

00:56:06.270 --> 00:56:08.690
who were sort of talking
about what comes next,

00:56:08.690 --> 00:56:11.400
that people wanted to move on.

00:56:11.400 --> 00:56:14.550
That if you went after Trump or the people

00:56:14.550 --> 00:56:16.073
who were trying to
overturn the government,

00:56:16.073 --> 00:56:19.140
everyone would see it as partisan

00:56:19.140 --> 00:56:22.623
and that, you know, just can't do that.

00:56:23.700 --> 00:56:25.800
But when the J6 hearing started,

00:56:25.800 --> 00:56:28.500
it turned out that's not
what people think, right?

00:56:28.500 --> 00:56:31.927
People actually want a
government that enforces the law,

00:56:31.927 --> 00:56:34.770
and that, in fact, over these last years

00:56:34.770 --> 00:56:36.687
when, you know, the banks, everybody else

00:56:36.687 --> 00:56:38.967
has been too big to jail, I think there's,

00:56:38.967 --> 00:56:41.757
and we see this in focus groups
and, like, a lot of things,

00:56:41.757 --> 00:56:44.370
people want accountability.

00:56:44.370 --> 00:56:46.998
And I think the, you know,

00:56:46.998 --> 00:56:50.190
if any of you had been asked

00:56:50.190 --> 00:56:53.370
if the Democrats held a J6 hearing

00:56:53.370 --> 00:56:57.390
the second week of October
and they raided Mar-a-Lago,

00:56:57.390 --> 00:56:59.947
whether everyone, voters would just think,

00:56:59.947 --> 00:57:01.560
"Oh, that's just partisan politics,"

00:57:01.560 --> 00:57:02.670
you'd say, "Of course."

00:57:02.670 --> 00:57:04.410
But that isn't what happened.

00:57:04.410 --> 00:57:06.360
And so what's gonna happen now

00:57:06.360 --> 00:57:09.513
because Republicans are
gonna do what Mo just said,

00:57:09.513 --> 00:57:12.150
is we are going to muddy this, right?

00:57:12.150 --> 00:57:16.104
They are going to take what
was coming to be a consensus

00:57:16.104 --> 00:57:19.083
about the unacceptability of what happened

00:57:19.083 --> 00:57:22.170
and create this confusion,

00:57:22.170 --> 00:57:25.320
continue the idea that there
was something illegitimate.

00:57:25.320 --> 00:57:27.272
And so, like, yeah, there's
a lot of stuff about,

00:57:27.272 --> 00:57:29.943
you know, passing bills
and all the other things.

00:57:29.943 --> 00:57:32.940
But we really almost had
a moment where we could

00:57:32.940 --> 00:57:34.728
get back on track on rule of law

00:57:34.728 --> 00:57:37.242
and a sort of coming together

00:57:37.242 --> 00:57:40.020
around accountability.

00:57:40.020 --> 00:57:41.583
And that's kind of shot.

00:57:43.643 --> 00:57:45.360
- I'll give the contrarian.

00:57:45.360 --> 00:57:46.710
I don't know if I believe
what I'm about to say,

00:57:46.710 --> 00:57:50.300
but I'll give a contrarian
optimistic take, which is...

00:57:52.200 --> 00:57:54.810
What we've been seeing over
the last couple of years

00:57:54.810 --> 00:57:57.530
is, as Lynn showed really good data on,

00:57:57.530 --> 00:57:59.800
is that people see differences
between the two parties.

00:57:59.800 --> 00:58:02.760
They recognize that when
you're voting for Democrats,

00:58:02.760 --> 00:58:04.707
you are getting Nancy
Pelosi or Chuck Schumer,

00:58:04.707 --> 00:58:06.840
and that means a certain
consolation of ideas.

00:58:06.840 --> 00:58:08.280
Or when you're voting for Republicans,

00:58:08.280 --> 00:58:10.830
you are voting for Kevin
McCarthy and Mitch McConnell,

00:58:10.830 --> 00:58:13.140
you're getting a certain
constellation of ideas

00:58:13.140 --> 00:58:14.970
and certain constellation of policies.

00:58:14.970 --> 00:58:17.637
And it means now that
voters are being guided more

00:58:17.637 --> 00:58:20.045
by these differences,
these policy differences

00:58:20.045 --> 00:58:22.290
between the parties and that, you know,

00:58:22.290 --> 00:58:23.723
maybe the optimistic take is that

00:58:23.723 --> 00:58:25.800
voters are gonna get what they want.

00:58:25.800 --> 00:58:27.551
They're going to get this

00:58:27.551 --> 00:58:30.090
policy that's reflective
of who they're voting for,

00:58:30.090 --> 00:58:31.749
because they now recognize the stakes

00:58:31.749 --> 00:58:34.152
of the policy stakes of
who they're voting for.

00:58:34.152 --> 00:58:36.933
They're no longer voting for
their local member of Congress

00:58:36.933 --> 00:58:40.380
because they're a good
guy or whatever it may be.

00:58:40.380 --> 00:58:41.700
They're now voting for
their member of Congress

00:58:41.700 --> 00:58:43.470
because they want Nancy Pelosi

00:58:43.470 --> 00:58:44.787
or because they want Kevin McCarthy.

00:58:44.787 --> 00:58:47.429
They're guided by these
policy motivations.

00:58:47.429 --> 00:58:49.197
Whether or not they end up getting that

00:58:49.197 --> 00:58:52.361
is limited by the bicameralism
and all the veto points

00:58:52.361 --> 00:58:53.565
in American politics.

00:58:53.565 --> 00:58:56.490
But I think the optimistic
take on voter behavior

00:58:56.490 --> 00:58:58.500
on individual voters
is that they're seeing

00:58:58.500 --> 00:58:59.490
these policy differences,

00:58:59.490 --> 00:59:01.440
these major differences
between the parties.

00:59:01.440 --> 00:59:03.607
And I think that could be encouraging.

00:59:03.607 --> 00:59:05.850
- I'll pick up on the optimism thread,

00:59:05.850 --> 00:59:07.140
'cause everybody always tells me

00:59:07.140 --> 00:59:09.963
that the calcification
argument is a real bummer.

00:59:11.130 --> 00:59:12.081
And just say that, Alan, like,

00:59:12.081 --> 00:59:15.320
you asked about policy and...

00:59:16.593 --> 00:59:18.594
The policies that affect people's lives

00:59:18.594 --> 00:59:21.477
can come from places other than Congress.

00:59:21.477 --> 00:59:24.000
So gridlock and Congress, for sure.

00:59:24.000 --> 00:59:25.980
Don't disagree with any of that.

00:59:25.980 --> 00:59:28.403
But the word that I would use to describe

00:59:28.403 --> 00:59:30.510
what kinds of policy environment people

00:59:30.510 --> 00:59:32.688
are gonna be living in is just whiplash.

00:59:32.688 --> 00:59:36.570
So you elect Donald Trump

00:59:36.570 --> 00:59:38.790
and maybe he writes a
bunch of executive orders,

00:59:38.790 --> 00:59:40.920
changes the world in one direction.

00:59:40.920 --> 00:59:43.170
You elect Joe Biden, he comes in,

00:59:43.170 --> 00:59:46.230
undoes all of that back
in the other direction.

00:59:46.230 --> 00:59:48.311
And so a lot, and they're so far apart,

00:59:48.311 --> 00:59:50.670
that for voters, I think a lot of what

00:59:50.670 --> 00:59:53.633
it's gonna feel like is whiplash.
It was this, it was that.

00:59:53.633 --> 00:59:56.220
Policy is devolving down to states.

00:59:56.220 --> 00:59:57.720
There's one policy in California,

00:59:57.720 --> 00:59:59.280
there's another policy in Ohio.

00:59:59.280 --> 01:00:01.200
So it's going to be very, you know,

01:00:01.200 --> 01:00:04.890
very different over time and space.

01:00:04.890 --> 01:00:06.191
And then on the democracy front,

01:00:06.191 --> 01:00:09.120
I would say that before
the midterm elections,

01:00:09.120 --> 01:00:13.031
I was worried that we would
see continued backsliding.

01:00:13.031 --> 01:00:15.708
People who lost elections
would not concede,

01:00:15.708 --> 01:00:17.550
they would call on their supporters

01:00:17.550 --> 01:00:21.591
to march on their state
capitol or some such.

01:00:21.591 --> 01:00:25.918
And the fact that we
saw very little of that,

01:00:25.918 --> 01:00:29.130
I think was a really important
moment for democracy.

01:00:29.130 --> 01:00:30.963
So also an optimistic note.

01:00:32.490 --> 01:00:33.720
- Great.

01:00:33.720 --> 01:00:36.236
I think this is a very interesting topic,

01:00:36.236 --> 01:00:39.186
and I just want to give the panelists

01:00:39.186 --> 01:00:42.210
who spoke early in the round

01:00:42.210 --> 01:00:44.760
to have the opportunity
to respond if they,

01:00:44.760 --> 01:00:48.480
and to add any other comments
they want to before we move

01:00:48.480 --> 01:00:51.903
to the audience questions.

01:00:54.240 --> 01:00:58.053
Seeing limited desire to do so,

01:00:58.890 --> 01:01:00.540
why don't we move on to the questions,

01:01:00.540 --> 01:01:02.280
and let me just remind our audience,

01:01:02.280 --> 01:01:04.105
just send along questions,

01:01:04.105 --> 01:01:07.101
and I'm gonna try and read the questions

01:01:07.101 --> 01:01:08.340
that you send along.

01:01:08.340 --> 01:01:13.320
So send them along in a
form that is friendly,

01:01:13.320 --> 01:01:14.790
friendly for that purpose.

01:01:14.790 --> 01:01:16.650
Okay, so let me just get started

01:01:16.650 --> 01:01:18.363
with some of those questions.

01:01:19.440 --> 01:01:21.173
So here's a question.

01:01:21.173 --> 01:01:23.010
I'd like to give Mike a chance

01:01:23.010 --> 01:01:25.560
to spin out his argument further.

01:01:25.560 --> 01:01:29.400
If the crucial question is
how a GOP that tripled down

01:01:29.400 --> 01:01:33.030
on Trumpism after January 6th

01:01:33.030 --> 01:01:35.190
still captured the House,

01:01:35.190 --> 01:01:37.009
how does a focus on turnout

01:01:37.009 --> 01:01:39.670
and a distinction between
the MAGA battlefield

01:01:39.670 --> 01:01:42.603
and other races answer that question?

01:01:43.500 --> 01:01:46.050
- Yeah, so, thanks.

01:01:46.050 --> 01:01:49.500
I think the framework

01:01:49.500 --> 01:01:52.200
that I'm coming at it from is that

01:01:52.200 --> 01:01:56.560
in November, 2020, as much
probably as any election ever,

01:01:56.560 --> 01:01:58.980
people understood that it was

01:01:58.980 --> 01:02:01.721
about four more years of MAGA or not.

01:02:01.721 --> 01:02:05.400
And when that happened,
81 million people voted

01:02:05.400 --> 01:02:07.830
for not four more years of MAGA.

01:02:07.830 --> 01:02:10.554
Which means that in almost
every race that mattered,

01:02:10.554 --> 01:02:14.130
there were more people
who had voted against MAGA

01:02:14.130 --> 01:02:17.310
than there were who had
voted for it, right?

01:02:17.310 --> 01:02:19.005
And so, to me,

01:02:19.005 --> 01:02:24.005
the MAGA will continue

01:02:24.570 --> 01:02:25.440
to be defeated

01:02:25.440 --> 01:02:29.492
to the extent that that group of voters

01:02:29.492 --> 01:02:34.140
understands that that's what
this election is about again.

01:02:34.140 --> 01:02:37.746
And if they don't, then
they're not habitual voters.

01:02:37.746 --> 01:02:40.323
They just sort of go back to not voting.

01:02:41.366 --> 01:02:44.220
And that's the problem.

01:02:44.220 --> 01:02:48.988
And this is where the asymmetry
in information environments

01:02:48.988 --> 01:02:51.905
is really undermining democracy

01:02:51.905 --> 01:02:55.680
and sort of our hopes, right?

01:02:55.680 --> 01:02:58.276
For the people who are
living in Fox world,

01:02:58.276 --> 01:03:01.770
they know every election is existential,

01:03:01.770 --> 01:03:04.343
that Democrats are
pedophiles and the devil,

01:03:04.343 --> 01:03:07.524
and why they have to
sort of go out and vote.

01:03:07.524 --> 01:03:11.520
And the mainstream media and
the people who are trusted

01:03:11.520 --> 01:03:13.560
wobble on it, right?

01:03:13.560 --> 01:03:18.560
And when, you know, over
June through mid-September

01:03:19.170 --> 01:03:23.220
when the mainstream media
was very much covering J6,

01:03:23.220 --> 01:03:25.590
Mar-a-Lago, Dobbs, all of that,

01:03:25.590 --> 01:03:27.960
you saw this big movement
towards Democrats

01:03:27.960 --> 01:03:31.050
and the generic and all of those things.

01:03:31.050 --> 01:03:34.347
These people are being
reminded what's at stake.

01:03:34.347 --> 01:03:36.390
And then you get the reversal

01:03:36.390 --> 01:03:38.340
where the media says, "Oh, no.

01:03:38.340 --> 01:03:39.810
People don't care about it anymore.

01:03:39.810 --> 01:03:41.244
We're gonna cover other stuff."

01:03:41.244 --> 01:03:46.244
And suddenly the air starts
coming out of the balloon.

01:03:46.830 --> 01:03:48.390
Right?

01:03:48.390 --> 01:03:49.660
But my point is that-

01:03:49.660 --> 01:03:51.019
- I was just gonna say, Mike.

01:03:51.019 --> 01:03:55.320
So I just wanna call your attention

01:03:55.320 --> 01:03:58.453
to one part of the question.

01:03:58.453 --> 01:04:01.447
The question of the differential patterns

01:04:01.447 --> 01:04:05.400
in the MAGA versus the non MAGA states,

01:04:05.400 --> 01:04:06.598
how that fit in.

01:04:06.598 --> 01:04:07.431
- Oh, yeah.

01:04:07.431 --> 01:04:08.850
Just that, what I was saying is that

01:04:08.850 --> 01:04:12.753
in the MAGA and non-MAGA-

01:04:12.753 --> 01:04:14.577
- I'm sorry, the MAGA battlefield states.

01:04:14.577 --> 01:04:16.020
- The MAGA war zone.

01:04:16.020 --> 01:04:19.916
MAGA war zone and the rest of the states,

01:04:19.916 --> 01:04:23.130
Republican House candidates in both places

01:04:23.130 --> 01:04:27.300
got about 3/4 of the
number of votes Trump got.

01:04:27.300 --> 01:04:30.380
And in the MAGA war zone,

01:04:30.380 --> 01:04:34.680
Democrats got about 73%,

01:04:34.680 --> 01:04:37.036
74, almost parity.

01:04:37.036 --> 01:04:40.890
And in the other areas, only 66%.

01:04:40.890 --> 01:04:43.305
And when we get the voter
file, we can parse that more,

01:04:43.305 --> 01:04:47.070
but the point is that there just weren't

01:04:47.070 --> 01:04:50.580
Democrats coming out in the same intensity

01:04:50.580 --> 01:04:53.220
in the rest of the country.

01:04:53.220 --> 01:04:55.740
And that's right,

01:04:55.740 --> 01:04:58.442
if they're not on full alert.

01:04:58.442 --> 01:05:01.310
And although, you know, one of the things,

01:05:01.310 --> 01:05:05.791
if you go back and look
at 2018 or 2014, whatever,

01:05:05.791 --> 01:05:08.491
you do not see these big regional,

01:05:08.491 --> 01:05:10.740
there's no regional difference

01:05:10.740 --> 01:05:12.522
between the competitive states

01:05:12.522 --> 01:05:14.690
and the non-competitive states

01:05:14.690 --> 01:05:16.418
in the previous presidential race

01:05:16.418 --> 01:05:19.980
and turn out in the midterm, right?

01:05:19.980 --> 01:05:22.050
It's just not sensitive to it.

01:05:22.050 --> 01:05:25.068
It's a new, different phenomenon.

01:05:25.068 --> 01:05:26.415
- Okay, great.

01:05:26.415 --> 01:05:28.620
Thank you very much.

01:05:28.620 --> 01:05:30.093
Okay, next question.

01:05:30.960 --> 01:05:32.783
Could the panelists address whether

01:05:32.783 --> 01:05:36.840
there are really demographic
shifts within the country

01:05:36.840 --> 01:05:39.903
that are going to impact
either party in the future?

01:05:42.180 --> 01:05:43.013
Okay, yeah.

01:05:43.013 --> 01:05:45.330
There's another part to the question.

01:05:45.330 --> 01:05:49.083
But let's start out with
demographics and voting patterns.

01:05:50.783 --> 01:05:53.277
I mean, I think that, you know,

01:05:53.277 --> 01:05:55.480
I think this is in the spirit of Teixeira

01:05:57.630 --> 01:06:01.950
arguments about, so
maybe, maybe Mo Fiorina,

01:06:01.950 --> 01:06:03.360
if you care to start.

01:06:03.360 --> 01:06:05.283
- Sure. I'd be happy to.

01:06:07.860 --> 01:06:10.170
One of the main pieces of the argument

01:06:10.170 --> 01:06:11.876
of the emerging Democratic majority

01:06:11.876 --> 01:06:15.990
is the growing number of the
immigrants, especially Latinos.

01:06:15.990 --> 01:06:18.300
And obviously it hasn't
worked out that way,

01:06:18.300 --> 01:06:21.930
but I wrote a piece in
"RealClearPolitics" on this.

01:06:21.930 --> 01:06:24.780
There's a really important
book by a demographer

01:06:24.780 --> 01:06:27.180
by the name Richard Alba
at Princeton, or no,

01:06:27.180 --> 01:06:29.190
he's published by Princeton Press.

01:06:29.190 --> 01:06:32.550
And you've probably all read
this about how we will have

01:06:32.550 --> 01:06:35.509
a non-white majority by 2045 or so.

01:06:35.509 --> 01:06:39.150
And the demographers point
out that that is really,

01:06:39.150 --> 01:06:41.375
that prediction is based
on a very strange way

01:06:41.375 --> 01:06:43.524
of counting whites and non-whites.

01:06:43.524 --> 01:06:46.500
And I have slides on
this, which I, (laughs)

01:06:46.500 --> 01:06:48.930
it probably would take me too
long to try to pull them up.

01:06:48.930 --> 01:06:52.664
But just to give an example,
Senator Cruz is half Cuban,

01:06:52.664 --> 01:06:55.800
so the census counts him as non-white.

01:06:55.800 --> 01:06:58.950
He has two daughters, his
wife, forget her name,

01:06:58.950 --> 01:07:00.960
but she's a northern European.

01:07:00.960 --> 01:07:03.660
He has two daughters who are 1/4 Cuban.

01:07:03.660 --> 01:07:05.880
They're counted in the
census as non-white.

01:07:05.880 --> 01:07:09.450
If the daughters have kids in 2045,

01:07:09.450 --> 01:07:12.540
and say they married totally, again,

01:07:12.540 --> 01:07:14.490
Norwegians or something like that,

01:07:14.490 --> 01:07:16.740
the kids who are 1/8 Cuban

01:07:16.740 --> 01:07:18.570
will be counted as non-white.

01:07:18.570 --> 01:07:21.780
That if you, it's basically
a Jim Crow in reverse

01:07:21.780 --> 01:07:22.613
that if you're counting,

01:07:22.613 --> 01:07:26.250
if you have anything other
than non-Hispanic white in you,

01:07:26.250 --> 01:07:29.310
you are counted as a person of color.

01:07:29.310 --> 01:07:32.940
And out of any kind of reasonable
way of counting people,

01:07:32.940 --> 01:07:35.189
it turns out that those
projections are way off,

01:07:35.189 --> 01:07:39.268
and they go on to show that
there's a tremendous amount

01:07:39.268 --> 01:07:41.520
of intermarriage going on.

01:07:41.520 --> 01:07:44.760
That, in other words, the
kind of examples I've given

01:07:44.760 --> 01:07:47.640
are what's actually occurring in reality.

01:07:47.640 --> 01:07:48.480
And the, you know,

01:07:48.480 --> 01:07:50.370
it's sort of politically
incorrect in some circles

01:07:50.370 --> 01:07:52.590
to talk about the great
American melting pot,

01:07:52.590 --> 01:07:54.745
but it's boiling away
as much as it ever did,

01:07:54.745 --> 01:07:57.150
especially with Hispanics.

01:07:57.150 --> 01:08:01.920
And so, the bottom line is neither party

01:08:01.920 --> 01:08:04.140
should basically base its stage

01:08:04.140 --> 01:08:06.828
on expectations of demography
delivering for you,

01:08:06.828 --> 01:08:09.240
because it's not gonna happen.

01:08:09.240 --> 01:08:12.162
What's gonna determine elections
is issues and performance

01:08:12.162 --> 01:08:13.923
as it has in the past.

01:08:15.150 --> 01:08:17.583
- So I am really happy for this question.

01:08:19.273 --> 01:08:22.770
Because this is something that, like,

01:08:22.770 --> 01:08:25.350
bothers me no end as someone who actually

01:08:25.350 --> 01:08:27.228
tries to win elections,

01:08:27.228 --> 01:08:30.450
is that if you are running a campaign,

01:08:30.450 --> 01:08:32.317
either, like, whatever you're doing,

01:08:32.317 --> 01:08:33.907
you're not sitting there thinking,

01:08:33.907 --> 01:08:37.281
"Well, like, how do I do
with this demographic group?"

01:08:37.281 --> 01:08:42.030
You're thinking, "How do I get
everybody who agrees with me

01:08:42.030 --> 01:08:43.650
to vote for me?"

01:08:43.650 --> 01:08:44.483
Right?

01:08:44.483 --> 01:08:48.060
And you're thinking about
there's no intermediate step

01:08:48.060 --> 01:08:51.357
of whether they're Latino
or a suburban voter

01:08:51.357 --> 01:08:53.790
or something like that, right?

01:08:53.790 --> 01:08:57.090
You are building a coalition of people

01:08:57.090 --> 01:09:00.223
who agree with different
parts of what you want to do.

01:09:00.223 --> 01:09:02.880
And it's incidental, right?

01:09:02.880 --> 01:09:06.279
It's descriptive statistics afterwards

01:09:06.279 --> 01:09:09.480
to say that it was because they were this

01:09:09.480 --> 01:09:11.610
or that demographic group, right?

01:09:11.610 --> 01:09:13.756
And there are only two
demographic groups, to me,

01:09:13.756 --> 01:09:16.560
that are large and matter

01:09:16.560 --> 01:09:21.560
where it actually operates in that way.

01:09:21.720 --> 01:09:24.502
And that's white evangelicals
and African-Americans.

01:09:24.502 --> 01:09:29.005
Where there's such a close,

01:09:29.005 --> 01:09:31.588
you know, to a 100% identity

01:09:31.588 --> 01:09:35.310
with what that demographic
name is, you know?

01:09:35.310 --> 01:09:38.670
But other than that, it's
just going back and counting

01:09:38.670 --> 01:09:41.501
the number of people in
each of these categories

01:09:41.501 --> 01:09:43.800
that ended up agreeing with you.

01:09:43.800 --> 01:09:46.590
And that's why, like,
after every election,

01:09:46.590 --> 01:09:49.350
people are, like, chasing these, like,

01:09:49.350 --> 01:09:52.860
you know, you know,
narrating a turtle race

01:09:52.860 --> 01:09:54.450
or something, right?

01:09:54.450 --> 01:09:56.850
Because that's not what's determining

01:09:56.850 --> 01:09:57.930
what they're gonna vote for.

01:09:57.930 --> 01:10:01.050
They're doing it on the basis of, like,

01:10:01.050 --> 01:10:04.023
how you put that coalition together.

01:10:07.860 --> 01:10:10.683
- Further thoughts on this question?

01:10:11.970 --> 01:10:14.010
Oh, or comments?

01:10:14.010 --> 01:10:14.843
Okay. Seeing none.

01:10:14.843 --> 01:10:16.905
- Actually, can I say
one other thing about it?

01:10:16.905 --> 01:10:17.738
- Yes, of course.

01:10:17.738 --> 01:10:19.350
- That is also, obviously
this is something

01:10:19.350 --> 01:10:22.163
that exercises me, is that,

01:10:22.163 --> 01:10:25.590
like many other things
in political analysis,

01:10:25.590 --> 01:10:28.807
especially in the press
and everything like that,

01:10:28.807 --> 01:10:32.670
there's just rampant
ecological fallacies, right?

01:10:32.670 --> 01:10:37.071
Where everybody says,
"Oh, pick the group."

01:10:37.071 --> 01:10:39.882
They just move this way
and everybody imagines

01:10:39.882 --> 01:10:42.483
a particular person in that group

01:10:42.483 --> 01:10:45.967
changing their mind rather than saying,

01:10:45.967 --> 01:10:49.560
"Well, the group of people in that group

01:10:49.560 --> 01:10:51.316
who voted this time is different."

01:10:51.316 --> 01:10:54.270
Both things are going on, right?

01:10:54.270 --> 01:10:57.187
And so it's, like, when
you have polls that say,

01:10:57.187 --> 01:10:59.926
so, you know, suddenly
now people care less

01:10:59.926 --> 01:11:04.080
about issue X or they
care more about issue X.

01:11:04.080 --> 01:11:06.525
You don't even know whether or
not the people who've changed

01:11:06.525 --> 01:11:09.731
their voting partisanship
or, like, their vote choice

01:11:09.731 --> 01:11:12.210
are among the group that changed their...

01:11:12.210 --> 01:11:13.043
Right?

01:11:13.043 --> 01:11:16.323
It's just, we're really
sloppy about that stuff.

01:11:19.170 --> 01:11:21.483
- Okay. Further thoughts?

01:11:22.410 --> 01:11:24.783
All right, next question.

01:11:25.860 --> 01:11:30.166
Which issues motivated
previously non-voters

01:11:30.166 --> 01:11:32.190
to go to the polls?

01:11:32.190 --> 01:11:35.993
And let me add to that
question a little bit.

01:11:35.993 --> 01:11:39.101
Thinking back, say to 2018,

01:11:39.101 --> 01:11:43.650
say, where we saw a really unusual,

01:11:43.650 --> 01:11:46.710
unusual event, maybe you would
say it at different times,

01:11:46.710 --> 01:11:49.376
but it was clearly
something unusual in 2018

01:11:49.376 --> 01:11:53.920
and it, so what do you think is going on

01:11:55.200 --> 01:12:00.000
with respect to some of the breaks

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:01.987
in the traditional turnout patterns

01:12:01.987 --> 01:12:06.987
that we observed over the
last half decade or so?

01:12:08.911 --> 01:12:10.773
- I'll just jump in quickly.

01:12:11.670 --> 01:12:14.070
I have not done a
systematic analysis of this,

01:12:14.070 --> 01:12:17.602
but my guess is that,
like, if I had to bet,

01:12:17.602 --> 01:12:22.020
I would bet my money on
that it isn't so much

01:12:22.020 --> 01:12:26.044
an issue as it is a sense among people

01:12:26.044 --> 01:12:29.340
that their vote is
gonna make a difference.

01:12:29.340 --> 01:12:32.040
So the election is gonna be close,

01:12:32.040 --> 01:12:34.530
and a lot is at stake, right?

01:12:34.530 --> 01:12:37.994
And so if you want me to describe issues

01:12:37.994 --> 01:12:40.366
that I think make people feel that way,

01:12:40.366 --> 01:12:43.560
they would be these identity-based issues.

01:12:43.560 --> 01:12:46.353
So in 2018, Alan, you mentioned,

01:12:47.370 --> 01:12:50.700
that's the aftermath
after the 2016 election,

01:12:50.700 --> 01:12:53.280
which was decided in the electoral college

01:12:53.280 --> 01:12:56.101
by 77,000 votes in three states.

01:12:56.101 --> 01:13:01.101
And a very surprising
outcome that nobody expected.

01:13:01.980 --> 01:13:04.027
And so in 2018 then
lots of people are like,

01:13:04.027 --> 01:13:08.577
"Oh man, you know, I better participate."

01:13:08.577 --> 01:13:11.245
And they're reacting to the change

01:13:11.245 --> 01:13:14.640
in the conversation to
these identity-based issues.

01:13:14.640 --> 01:13:16.503
And then again, in 2022,

01:13:16.503 --> 01:13:19.770
in 2020 it's, you know, these,

01:13:19.770 --> 01:13:21.450
everyone knows the elections
are gonna be close,

01:13:21.450 --> 01:13:23.927
it was closer than 2016.

01:13:23.927 --> 01:13:25.950
44,000 votes across three states

01:13:25.950 --> 01:13:28.650
determine the 2020 presidential election.

01:13:28.650 --> 01:13:31.830
And so that gets us a
boost in turnout in 2022.

01:13:31.830 --> 01:13:35.490
So I think it's not just one
issue makes people go vote.

01:13:35.490 --> 01:13:38.596
It's this sense of there's a lot at stake

01:13:38.596 --> 01:13:41.100
and, "Man, these
elections are really close

01:13:41.100 --> 01:13:42.270
and my vote might really matter."

01:13:42.270 --> 01:13:43.560
- Yeah.

01:13:43.560 --> 01:13:44.519
And also on this one,

01:13:44.519 --> 01:13:48.060
I have a very empirical answer,

01:13:48.060 --> 01:13:49.673
because we have it on the voter file.

01:13:49.673 --> 01:13:53.340
The first thing is that this election

01:13:53.340 --> 01:13:56.255
will have very few non-voters coming out.

01:13:56.255 --> 01:13:59.096
There are very few folks who decided

01:13:59.096 --> 01:14:02.733
that '20 wasn't important
enough, but this one was.

01:14:04.170 --> 01:14:09.170
But in '18, the big
boost was really among,

01:14:09.955 --> 01:14:12.900
one of the big hidden
ones that you can't see

01:14:12.900 --> 01:14:17.900
without a voter file is
pre-presidential Democratic voters

01:14:18.311 --> 01:14:22.380
who decided, "This is a
midterm I need to vote in."

01:14:22.380 --> 01:14:25.615
So it wasn't so much
people coming outta nowhere

01:14:25.615 --> 01:14:27.733
and deciding to vote.

01:14:27.733 --> 01:14:30.148
It was people who had
actually been pretty regular

01:14:30.148 --> 01:14:31.771
presidential year voters

01:14:31.771 --> 01:14:36.771
deciding in all of the
hothouse anti-Trump stuff

01:14:36.903 --> 01:14:40.503
that they better come out
and vote in 2018 as well.

01:14:43.560 --> 01:14:46.380
- Just one other observation that

01:14:46.380 --> 01:14:48.679
although the individual issues might

01:14:48.679 --> 01:14:51.312
affect a lot of the, you
know, election swings

01:14:51.312 --> 01:14:54.510
in terms of just this being
a much more high turnout era,

01:14:54.510 --> 01:14:56.052
that also goes back to the parties

01:14:56.052 --> 01:14:58.043
rediscovering get out the vote

01:14:58.043 --> 01:15:00.720
and suddenly being in the early 2000s

01:15:00.720 --> 01:15:02.671
with the Republican's 24/7 project

01:15:02.671 --> 01:15:04.920
and in proceeding, there's
just been a lot more

01:15:04.920 --> 01:15:07.380
mobilization effort in
the last two decades

01:15:07.380 --> 01:15:09.630
than there was even a
couple decades earlier.

01:15:12.660 --> 01:15:13.493
- There you go.

01:15:14.670 --> 01:15:16.021
Okay.

01:15:16.021 --> 01:15:20.103
Any more observations on
that before we move on?

01:15:21.232 --> 01:15:24.179
Okay.

01:15:24.179 --> 01:15:25.860
Here's a question.

01:15:25.860 --> 01:15:28.309
A year ago, David Brooks observed

01:15:28.309 --> 01:15:30.777
that the issue dividing the parties

01:15:30.777 --> 01:15:34.140
was shifting from pro versus con,

01:15:34.140 --> 01:15:37.590
the role of government
that has dominated politics

01:15:37.590 --> 01:15:42.183
since the New Deal to America
first versus globalism.

01:15:43.080 --> 01:15:45.633
Do you see any evidence
that this is occurring?

01:15:49.320 --> 01:15:50.153
So...

01:15:52.080 --> 01:15:53.163
Lynn, you're on mute.

01:15:54.840 --> 01:15:57.030
- Sorry, it's my first Zoom.

01:15:57.030 --> 01:16:00.510
Yeah, sounds a lot like
calcification to me.

01:16:00.510 --> 01:16:01.576
Not exactly the same idea,

01:16:01.576 --> 01:16:04.290
but this idea that there's a shifting away

01:16:04.290 --> 01:16:07.233
from this New Deal dimension
onto something else.

01:16:09.120 --> 01:16:12.000
- You mentioned the idea
that, I'm sorry, Josh,

01:16:12.000 --> 01:16:15.150
why don't you go and then
I had a follow up for Lynn.

01:16:15.150 --> 01:16:18.030
- Yeah, I don't know if,
like, Brooks' characterization

01:16:18.030 --> 01:16:20.837
of globalism is right for
the current dimension.

01:16:20.837 --> 01:16:25.110
I mean, we see on free trade
that Joe Biden, for example,

01:16:25.110 --> 01:16:27.540
is very much continuing Donald trade,

01:16:27.540 --> 01:16:29.910
Donald Trump's anti-free trade policies.

01:16:29.910 --> 01:16:32.790
And on the Inflation Reduction Act,

01:16:32.790 --> 01:16:35.970
Joe Biden is very much
focused on build in America,

01:16:35.970 --> 01:16:37.830
subsidize American production

01:16:37.830 --> 01:16:41.130
and American first kind
of manufacturing policy.

01:16:41.130 --> 01:16:44.190
So while there might be some of this shift

01:16:44.190 --> 01:16:45.720
away from New Deal,

01:16:45.720 --> 01:16:46.980
I don't know if the dimension

01:16:46.980 --> 01:16:49.246
is American first versus globalism.

01:16:49.246 --> 01:16:51.932
Also, I do wonder, Lynn, what we'll see

01:16:51.932 --> 01:16:54.870
in the next two years with Republicans

01:16:54.870 --> 01:16:56.220
as they control the House,

01:16:56.220 --> 01:16:59.514
whether or not they bring back
a focus on New Deal issues,

01:16:59.514 --> 01:17:02.160
on tax cuts and Obamacare.

01:17:02.160 --> 01:17:03.980
Like, what is a Kevin McCarthy
House going to look like

01:17:03.980 --> 01:17:06.480
when it comes to spending bills?

01:17:06.480 --> 01:17:07.313
- Yeah.

01:17:11.160 --> 01:17:12.213
- Further thoughts?

01:17:14.190 --> 01:17:16.503
Okay, let's see.

01:17:20.130 --> 01:17:22.710
Okay, according to election exit polls,

01:17:22.710 --> 01:17:24.448
Trump was more of a factor

01:17:24.448 --> 01:17:27.570
than former presidents usually are.

01:17:27.570 --> 01:17:31.394
Carries a strong negative, net negative.

01:17:31.394 --> 01:17:33.960
Was he and his (indistinct)
of election deniers

01:17:33.960 --> 01:17:37.230
the determining element
in the close election

01:17:37.230 --> 01:17:40.893
and therefore the partisan
divide in both houses?

01:17:42.596 --> 01:17:44.164
I think you sort of, yeah,

01:17:44.164 --> 01:17:45.920
we heard a little about that, I think,

01:17:45.920 --> 01:17:47.430
in the various presentations.

01:17:47.430 --> 01:17:49.470
Please, Lynn?

01:17:49.470 --> 01:17:50.700
- Well, I was just gonna say, like,

01:17:50.700 --> 01:17:54.644
don't just, I mean, not
every election is close.

01:17:54.644 --> 01:17:56.468
But in the ones that are close enough

01:17:56.468 --> 01:18:00.595
for something like the legacy
of Trump to make a difference,

01:18:00.595 --> 01:18:03.570
anything is making a difference, right?

01:18:03.570 --> 01:18:08.570
So, like, don't try to not
think of it's one thing.

01:18:08.670 --> 01:18:11.103
Like, it's all of these things.

01:18:12.330 --> 01:18:14.640
You know, not in every race,

01:18:14.640 --> 01:18:17.040
but in the ones where you think one thing

01:18:17.040 --> 01:18:19.950
could have made the difference,
it's not just one thing.

01:18:19.950 --> 01:18:21.903
It's gonna be a lot of these things.

01:18:23.970 --> 01:18:25.983
- Okay. Further thoughts on that?

01:18:28.170 --> 01:18:29.670
- Yeah, no, go ahead.

01:18:29.670 --> 01:18:30.526
- Please, Mike.

01:18:30.526 --> 01:18:31.950
- We've been on this for a while

01:18:31.950 --> 01:18:33.180
and I'm taking too much time.

01:18:33.180 --> 01:18:34.170
Go ahead.

01:18:34.170 --> 01:18:37.230
- Okay, so here's another question.

01:18:37.230 --> 01:18:41.010
What influence will voter
suppression measures

01:18:41.010 --> 01:18:43.530
have on the 2024 election?

01:18:43.530 --> 01:18:45.830
Do you think that will
be an important factor?

01:18:48.480 --> 01:18:50.490
- So I think that,

01:18:50.490 --> 01:18:52.200
I mean, it depends on
what you're gonna define

01:18:52.200 --> 01:18:54.313
as voter suppression and sort of the,

01:18:54.313 --> 01:18:58.305
I think that one of the
problems with the way

01:18:58.305 --> 01:19:03.218
we study the anti-voting,
anti-democracy laws

01:19:03.218 --> 01:19:05.550
is we look at them, like,

01:19:05.550 --> 01:19:09.486
will this one provision change this thing

01:19:09.486 --> 01:19:12.432
rather than what the accumulation

01:19:12.432 --> 01:19:15.909
of Shelby, Citizens
United, Brnovich, Rucho,

01:19:15.909 --> 01:19:19.260
and everything else has done to change

01:19:19.260 --> 01:19:22.410
in an unrecognizable way
the way elections happen

01:19:22.410 --> 01:19:24.870
in the United States, right?

01:19:24.870 --> 01:19:29.870
And the one to the point, right,

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:31.408
go back to the idea of, like,

01:19:31.408 --> 01:19:33.997
there's insurrection
and no one even thinks,

01:19:33.997 --> 01:19:36.802
"Well, will they be held
accountable at the polling places?"

01:19:36.802 --> 01:19:38.510
Considered that

01:19:38.510 --> 01:19:43.510
of the 90 Republicans who ran,

01:19:43.890 --> 01:19:46.058
who voted against the electoral college

01:19:46.058 --> 01:19:48.998
and were running in states where

01:19:48.998 --> 01:19:52.020
Republicans drew the districts,

01:19:52.020 --> 01:19:56.400
only one was not in a safe seat, right?

01:19:56.400 --> 01:19:59.040
Voters never got a chance

01:19:59.040 --> 01:20:02.820
to hold accountable them for this.

01:20:02.820 --> 01:20:05.432
And there's a way in which the
sort of public discussion is,

01:20:05.432 --> 01:20:06.870
"Well, it must be fair

01:20:06.870 --> 01:20:10.350
because neither party has an advantage."

01:20:10.350 --> 01:20:13.806
But democracy and voters
don't have an advantage

01:20:13.806 --> 01:20:17.970
if we don't get to actually
exercise a choice in elections,

01:20:17.970 --> 01:20:21.540
especially after such a
momentous event, right?

01:20:21.540 --> 01:20:25.140
And that doesn't get measured
in, like, a difference,

01:20:25.140 --> 01:20:28.560
a different study on this
voter provision, right?

01:20:28.560 --> 01:20:31.590
But the in the aggregate, you-

01:20:31.590 --> 01:20:34.110
- I was gonna say, we actually
just saw such a paper.

01:20:34.110 --> 01:20:39.110
Josh and I just saw such a
paper this very day. (laughs)

01:20:39.160 --> 01:20:42.393
- But the point is, that,
like, there's stuff that,

01:20:43.663 --> 01:20:46.036
like, you can just see
from outer space, right?

01:20:46.036 --> 01:20:49.498
And in terms of how American
elections have changed.

01:20:49.498 --> 01:20:54.498
And it's the foundation for why we have,

01:20:54.888 --> 01:20:58.473
you know, a MAGA House of Representatives.

01:20:59.640 --> 01:21:02.610
- Let me ask one last question,

01:21:02.610 --> 01:21:05.160
because we're getting close
to the end of our time.

01:21:06.090 --> 01:21:07.410
This is a question.

01:21:07.410 --> 01:21:09.087
This program is great,

01:21:09.087 --> 01:21:12.480
and I would love to get beyond
what I read in the press.

01:21:12.480 --> 01:21:15.300
Would the panelists give
us a few academic papers

01:21:15.300 --> 01:21:18.270
to pull and read and let me just say,

01:21:18.270 --> 01:21:22.320
also maybe a recommendation or website

01:21:22.320 --> 01:21:26.020
or places to go for commentary

01:21:26.020 --> 01:21:29.490
and news that they would recommend

01:21:29.490 --> 01:21:33.510
as especially insightful

01:21:33.510 --> 01:21:35.636
and maybe a different voice

01:21:35.636 --> 01:21:37.360
than you would hear

01:21:38.431 --> 01:21:41.880
and that would allow you to get beyond

01:21:41.880 --> 01:21:43.511
what I read in the press.

01:21:43.511 --> 01:21:45.700
I guess it is, of course the press.

01:21:45.700 --> 01:21:48.120
But you know what I mean.

01:21:48.120 --> 01:21:50.073
Okay, any ideas, friends?

01:21:52.447 --> 01:21:55.920
- "The Bitter End" by Sides,
Tausanovitch and Vavreck,

01:21:55.920 --> 01:21:57.840
recently came out by
Princeton University Press

01:21:57.840 --> 01:21:59.490
and it's a fantastic book,

01:21:59.490 --> 01:22:00.930
as well as Mo's work.

01:22:00.930 --> 01:22:01.838
- Thank you, Josh.

01:22:01.838 --> 01:22:04.260
- Mo, is "Unstable Majorities"
your most recent book?

01:22:04.260 --> 01:22:05.910
Am I getting that one right?

01:22:05.910 --> 01:22:07.080
- Yes.

01:22:07.080 --> 01:22:09.030
- So "The Bitter End"
and "Unstable Majorities"

01:22:09.030 --> 01:22:11.618
by two of our panelists are
fantastic books to read.

01:22:11.618 --> 01:22:13.848
(Lynn chuckles)

01:22:13.848 --> 01:22:15.480
- Well, thank you, Josh.

01:22:15.480 --> 01:22:16.313
- Thank you.

01:22:16.313 --> 01:22:19.320
If you want commentary
on day-to-day politics,

01:22:19.320 --> 01:22:22.680
I mentioned Ruy Teixeira
as the Liberal Patriot.

01:22:22.680 --> 01:22:24.900
There are several readers
or several writers,

01:22:24.900 --> 01:22:27.660
but he is particularly
good on just an analysis

01:22:27.660 --> 01:22:29.732
sort of in between the
academic political science

01:22:29.732 --> 01:22:31.110
and journalism.

01:22:31.110 --> 01:22:33.532
And, you know, I just think,

01:22:33.532 --> 01:22:38.532
well, look at it every
time he writes something.

01:22:38.560 --> 01:22:40.950
- Other recommendations, please.

01:22:40.950 --> 01:22:42.030
- I was just gonna say,

01:22:42.030 --> 01:22:46.172
I asked my graduate students
in our last meeting,

01:22:46.172 --> 01:22:50.989
this quarter, what the best
thing was that they read

01:22:50.989 --> 01:22:55.523
all quarter long, and then they
made me participate as well.

01:22:55.523 --> 01:22:58.230
And so I'm gonna share with you the paper

01:22:58.230 --> 01:23:00.750
that I read this quarter.

01:23:00.750 --> 01:23:02.940
So that doesn't mean it just came out,

01:23:02.940 --> 01:23:04.843
but it's when I read it.

01:23:04.843 --> 01:23:08.013
It's a paper actually by some Yale folks.

01:23:09.600 --> 01:23:11.580
I don't know how to say
this woman's first name.

01:23:11.580 --> 01:23:13.983
I think it, is it Lila or Lilla Orr?

01:23:14.880 --> 01:23:19.860
O-R-R, and our friend and
your colleague Greg Huber.

01:23:19.860 --> 01:23:22.230
And this is a paper that is in,

01:23:22.230 --> 01:23:24.090
I think it's in the "American
Political Science Review,"

01:23:24.090 --> 01:23:28.270
and it is about sorting out
how much of people's reactions

01:23:28.270 --> 01:23:31.443
to the parties are purely based on emotion

01:23:31.443 --> 01:23:36.360
and how much of it has a
policy component to it.

01:23:36.360 --> 01:23:38.040
And it's not a mystery novel,

01:23:38.040 --> 01:23:39.570
so I'll just give away the finding,

01:23:39.570 --> 01:23:42.840
is that a good deal of people's reactions,

01:23:42.840 --> 01:23:44.314
emotional reactions to the party

01:23:44.314 --> 01:23:46.398
or to people's party labels,

01:23:46.398 --> 01:23:49.620
comes from the beliefs
about what they wanna do

01:23:49.620 --> 01:23:50.550
in the world.

01:23:50.550 --> 01:23:52.680
So we hear, you know, "Oh,
like, I don't want my son

01:23:52.680 --> 01:23:54.240
or daughter to marry a Republican

01:23:54.240 --> 01:23:55.247
or marry a Democrat."

01:23:55.247 --> 01:23:57.150
"Ah!" And it's emotion.

01:23:57.150 --> 01:24:00.060
But what they sort out is
that actually a lot of that

01:24:00.060 --> 01:24:01.980
is because you wouldn't want your child

01:24:01.980 --> 01:24:04.471
to marry someone who holds those beliefs.

01:24:04.471 --> 01:24:06.060
And it isn't just emotional.

01:24:06.060 --> 01:24:08.613
It's a really, really
creative and nice paper.

01:24:09.840 --> 01:24:10.950
- I'll give another answer as well,

01:24:10.950 --> 01:24:13.670
which is read political science journals.

01:24:13.670 --> 01:24:15.300
So I mean, some of our journals

01:24:15.300 --> 01:24:17.130
are the "American
Political Science Review,"

01:24:17.130 --> 01:24:19.770
the "American Journal
of Political Science."

01:24:19.770 --> 01:24:22.560
Look at the abstracts, look
at the table of contents.

01:24:22.560 --> 01:24:24.030
Don't pay for the articles,

01:24:24.030 --> 01:24:26.040
but if you see an article
that really interests you,

01:24:26.040 --> 01:24:28.770
email the author and those
authors are always happy

01:24:28.770 --> 01:24:30.120
to send those PDFs to folks.

01:24:30.120 --> 01:24:32.400
So if you see a paper that interests you,

01:24:32.400 --> 01:24:34.380
feel free to reach out.

01:24:34.380 --> 01:24:36.630
Don't send long rambling
emails to academics,

01:24:36.630 --> 01:24:37.630
but if you want, like, a little PDF,

01:24:37.630 --> 01:24:39.843
we're more than happy to share those PDFs.

01:24:41.760 --> 01:24:43.432
- Right, so I think we're gonna see a run

01:24:43.432 --> 01:24:45.311
on the "American
Political Science Review."

01:24:45.311 --> 01:24:46.350
- I was just gonna say.

01:24:46.350 --> 01:24:49.739
I already sent a field
experiment from Alan

01:24:49.739 --> 01:24:51.853
about emailing random political scientists

01:24:51.853 --> 01:24:55.477
to see if they'll send
PDFs to people. (chuckles)

01:24:55.477 --> 01:24:57.360
- Did I? Okay. (laughs)

01:24:57.360 --> 01:24:59.100
- Who's more likely to respond?

01:24:59.100 --> 01:25:00.576
- Well, I'm sure that's,

01:25:00.576 --> 01:25:03.030
some version of that has been done

01:25:03.030 --> 01:25:05.820
and many times, I assume.

01:25:05.820 --> 01:25:08.051
By all sorts of people,
including graduate students

01:25:08.051 --> 01:25:10.800
and undergraduates.

01:25:10.800 --> 01:25:12.960
So all very good.

01:25:12.960 --> 01:25:15.310
Mike, do you have anything
you wanna recommend?

01:25:16.230 --> 01:25:17.400
- I don't think so. No.

01:25:17.400 --> 01:25:20.310
I love not just "The Bitter End,"

01:25:20.310 --> 01:25:21.824
but the books before it.

01:25:21.824 --> 01:25:22.973
- Oh, thank you.

01:25:22.973 --> 01:25:24.829
- I think they're the
best by volume recaps

01:25:24.829 --> 01:25:26.340
of all of the things.

01:25:26.340 --> 01:25:29.970
And I think one of the things that I think

01:25:29.970 --> 01:25:31.980
is really great about them,

01:25:31.980 --> 01:25:35.163
is that you,

01:25:36.590 --> 01:25:39.330
is the way you take different topics

01:25:39.330 --> 01:25:42.515
that are, were of interest
during the election

01:25:42.515 --> 01:25:45.435
and find a way to explore them

01:25:45.435 --> 01:25:49.770
in a smart, data-based way.

01:25:49.770 --> 01:25:50.603
- Thanks.

01:25:51.510 --> 01:25:54.000
- Which usually is never the case

01:25:54.000 --> 01:25:56.777
when they're discussed in public.

01:25:56.777 --> 01:26:00.513
And so I thought those were really good.

01:26:01.670 --> 01:26:02.512
- Great.
- Thank you.

01:26:02.512 --> 01:26:03.345
- Terrific.

01:26:03.345 --> 01:26:04.749
- I did think of-
- Please.

01:26:04.749 --> 01:26:05.653
- I thought of a podcast,

01:26:05.653 --> 01:26:08.262
I thought, just my
favorite political podcast

01:26:08.262 --> 01:26:13.262
is "Hacks on Tap," which is
David Axelrod and Mike Murphy.

01:26:14.670 --> 01:26:16.514
And they are not doing policy,

01:26:16.514 --> 01:26:19.560
but they are talking about
elections and strategy

01:26:19.560 --> 01:26:20.393
and the kinds of things

01:26:20.393 --> 01:26:22.980
that campaign consultants talk about.

01:26:22.980 --> 01:26:24.450
And so if you like that kind of thing,

01:26:24.450 --> 01:26:25.740
which it sounds like from the Q&amp;A.

01:26:25.740 --> 01:26:29.071
people sort of do, I recommend it.

01:26:29.071 --> 01:26:31.440
It's funny and it's all,

01:26:31.440 --> 01:26:34.800
it's weekly and super relevant.

01:26:34.800 --> 01:26:36.750
- Yeah, actually I'll do one other thing,

01:26:36.750 --> 01:26:39.120
but which is just category,
more of a category

01:26:39.120 --> 01:26:44.120
that I think is really underappreciated,

01:26:44.151 --> 01:26:49.151
is what I've seen in new attempts

01:26:49.635 --> 01:26:52.120
to have

01:26:53.250 --> 01:26:55.590
analysis of political economy

01:26:55.590 --> 01:26:59.490
where it's not just about one more person

01:26:59.490 --> 01:27:02.490
doing or on surveys,

01:27:02.490 --> 01:27:06.060
but really understanding
things like path dependency

01:27:06.060 --> 01:27:10.830
in our country and the different
ways, economic interests,

01:27:10.830 --> 01:27:12.600
all those kinds of things.

01:27:12.600 --> 01:27:15.480
And as kind of an old guy, right?

01:27:15.480 --> 01:27:18.750
Like, that used to be the
way people understood things.

01:27:18.750 --> 01:27:23.103
And I think if it's making a
comeback, that's a great thing.

01:27:23.940 --> 01:27:25.230
- Great. Well, thank you.

01:27:25.230 --> 01:27:26.838
Thank you all.

